Welsh councillors are concerned about "looking like a fool"
There are some councillors involved in a proposal to have their title changed to MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
Worries have arisen as a result of a plan to change the council's name to the Welsh Parliament.
Stewards from all parties are worried that it could make them ridicule.
One Labour official said his party was concerned that "it rhymes with Twp and Pwp."
For readers living outside Wales: in Welsh, twp means mad and pwp means faeces.
A councillor from Plaid Cymru's party said the party as a whole was "dissatisfied" and had come up with other proposals.
A sworter from the Welsh Conservative Party said his party had an "open mind" about the name change, but pointed out that there was not much difference between the word MWP and Muppet.
In this context, it can be noted that the Welsh letter w is pronounced similar to u in Icelandic.
The council, which is currently working on a legislative proposal to propose the name changes, said: "The final decision on all the names held over members of the council will of course lie with the members themselves."
Legislation on the Government of the Wales Act 2017 gave the Welsh Council the power to change its name.
In June, the council released the results of an opinion poll among the public that showed strong support for calling the council the Welsh parliament.
In terms of the council's title, the Welsh Parliament (Welsh Members Welsh MPs) chose WMP, but the MWP received the most support from the public in the polls.
According to news reports, councillors now submit other proposals, but it may be difficult to reach an agreement for the council's chairman, Elin Jones, who is due to submit a bill on the changes within a few weeks.
The reform bill will also include other changes to the way the council operates, including rules on the expulsion of councillors and the organisation of the committee system.
Board members will be able to cast a final vote on their title when discussions on the bill begin.
The Macedonians vote in elections to change the country's name
Voters will vote on Sunday on whether to change their country's name to "Northern Macedonia's Republic."
This case was put to a vote in an attempt to resolve a decades-long disagreement with neighboring Greece, which has its own province with the name Macedonia.
Athena city has long argued that the names of their neighbours in the north is equivalent to a claim on its territory and has repeatedly objected to their applications for membership of the EU and NATO.
Macedonia's president Gjorge Ivanov, who disagrees with the referendum on the name change, has said he won't regard the election as valid.
Supporters of the vote, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, have, however, said the name change is simply the price that needs to be paid in order to gain membership of the EU and Nato.
St. Martin's church bells are silent as churches in Harlem are struggling
“The older people I’ve spoken to said that in the past there’s a bar and a church on every street corner.”
"There's nowhere to be found there."
He said it's understandable that bars would be declining.
"People have other ways to meet," he said.
“Bars are no longer the centers of the neighborhood where people come together on a regular basis.”
As far as the churches are concerned, he's concerned that the money earned from the sale of assets won't last as long as leaders expect "and sooner or later they'll be back to the beginning."
He added that it is possible that churches would be replaced by residential buildings that are full of people who will not assist in rescuing the remaining refuges in the surrounding area.
“By far the majority of people who buy apartments in these buildings will be white,” he said, “and as a result it will speed up the fact that these churches will be permanently closed, because it’s unlikely that most of the people who move into these apartments will become members of these churches.”
Both churches were built by white congregations before Harlem became the center of black - the Metropolitan Community was built in 1870, and St. Martin’s church a decade later.
The original White Methodist congregation evacuated in the 1930s.
A congregation of black people who had worked nearby took over the building.
Under the guidance of Reverend John Howard Johnson, a member of the Black Congregation took over St. Martin’s Church, who took the initiative to circumvent shopkeepers on 125th Street, Harlem’s main shopping street, who longed to hire black workers or give them promotions.
The building was severely damaged by a fire in 1939, but members of Reverend Johnson's congregation made plans to rebuild it and they also had the bell game built.
Pastor David Johnson, son and successor to Reverend Johnson, called the bells a proud "clock of the poor."
The expert who played the bell game in July called it "Cultivary treasure" and "a unique historical instrument."
Tiffany Ng from the University of Michigan also stated that this was the first clock game in the world that a black musician, Dionisio A. Lind, played on, but he moved on to a larger clock game at Riverside church 18 years ago.
Mr Merriweather said St. Martin did not get anyone else to replace him.
Over the past few months, numerous architects and contractors have made their way to St. Martin, some recruited by church leaders and others by the Diocese.
The Church Council - which oversees the parish and is composed of church leaders - wrote to the Diocese in July and expressed its concern that the Diocese "would seek to transfer the costs to" the Church Council, even if it had not been involved in the recruitment of the architects and contractors sent by the Diocese.
Some parishioners complained about the lack of transparency on the part of the Diocese.
Shark wound 13-year-old boy who dived after lobster in California
According to local authorities, a shark attacked a 13-year-old boy and wounded him on Saturday when the boy was diving for lobster in California on the first day of the lobster hunting season.
The attack occurred just before seven o’clock in the morning near Beacon’s Beach in Encinitas.
Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV in San Diego that he's been diving along with friends for about half an hour on Saturday morning when he heard the boy shout for help and then rowed over along with a group of others to help get him out of the water.
Hammel said he initially thought it was purely a thrill to have reached lobster, but then “he realized that the boy shouted “I was bitten!
“I’ve got bitten!”
"His entire collarbone was ripped up," Hammel said of what he saw when he approached the boy.
“I said, ‘There’s a shark in the water!’” added Hammel.
The boy was taken by helicopter to Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego where he lies seriously wounded.
It is not known of what type the shark is that attacked the boy.
Coast Guard chief Larry Giles said at a media briefing that a shark had been spotted in the area a few weeks earlier, but he wasn't of a more dangerous type of shark.
Giles added that the victim had suffered serious damage to the upper body.
Authorities blocked coastal access from Ponto Beach Beach in Casablad to Swami’s beach in Encinitas for 48 hours for research and security purposes.
Giles said there were over 135 different types of sharks in the area, but most of them are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury's plan to gain ground in Britain's cosmetics market
Sainsbury’s plans to compete with Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams with store-style beauty shelves and dedicated support staff.
This is part of a plan to enter Britain's cosmetics market, which is 2.8 billion pounds worth and is increasing while sales of fashion and household goods are declining. Larger cosmetic shelves will be tested in 11 stores across the country and will be installed in more stores next year if successful.
Investing in cosmetics is part of the way for supermarkets to use shelf space that was previously utilized by TVs, microwaves and household products.
Sainsbury’s says he plans to double its cosmetic range to up to 3000 products, including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw for the first time.
Current products from L’Oreal, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees will also get increased space with marked areas similar to those found in stores such as Boots.
The store also intends to restart the sale of its Boutique-cosmetic selection so that the majority of the products are vegan-friendly, but younger consumers are increasingly demanding.
In addition, Fragrance Shop will try out a product stand in Sainsbury’s two stores, the first one opened in Croydon in southern London last week and the next one will open in Selly Oak, Birmingham later this year.
An online store and the increased tendency of consumers to buy small amounts of food daily in neighborhood stores means that supermarkets need to work harder to get people to come to themselves.
Mike Coupe, Sainsbury’s chief executive, said the stores would resemble shopping malls to an increasing extent as the retail chain tries to combat the low-cost stores Aldi and Lidl with more services and products other than food.
Sainsbury’s has placed Argos stores in hundreds of stores and has also introduced a number of Habitat stores since both chains were bought two years ago. According to them, it has increased sales and made the purchase more profitable.
The store's first attempt to enliven its cosmetics and pharmaceutical departments was not successful.
Sainsbury’s tested a partnership with Boots around 2000, but it ended due to disagreements on how to share the income from the sale of drugs in the supermarkets.
The new strategy comes after Sainsbury’s sold its 281 pharmacies to Celesio, the owner of Lloyd’s drug stores, at 125 million pounds three years ago.
According to the policy, Lloyds will play a role in these programs by adding a large selection of luxury skincare brands, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy, to four stores.
Paull Mills-Hicks, Sainsbury’s chief marketing officer, said: “We’ve revolutionized the look of our cosmetics shelves to improve the environment for our customers.
We have also invested in special training for employees who will be available for advice.
Our brand selection is designed to meet all needs. Fascinating environments and convenient locations make us an exciting cosmetics store that transforms old shopping habits.”
