See: Daniel Sturridge, the Liverpool player, equalises against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge saved Liverpool from losing against Chelsea in the Premier League by scoring in the 89th minute of Saturday's game at Stamford Bridge Stadium in London.
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri about 30 yards away from Chelsea's goal as his team were under 1-0.
He rolled the ball to the left and then attempted a shot to goal towards the far post.
The ball flew high over the goal and towards its right corner.
The ball eventually crossed Kepa Arrizabalaga, who jumped up, and into the net.
"I just wanted to try to get into that position, to catch the ball, and players like Shaq always play it as far as possible so I tried to create as much time as I could," Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
“I saw Kante come and touch once and I didn’t think so much about it, just took the shot.”
Chelsea were over 1-0 at half-time after Belgian star Eden Hazard scored in the 25th minute.
On offense, the Chelsea striker gave the ball with a heel kick to Mateo Kovacic and then sprinted from midfield to Liverpool's half.
Kovacic let the ball move quickly in between in midfield.
Then he sent the ball impressive forward and sent Hazard into the penalty area.
Hazard ran the defenders away and scored into the far corner with a left-foot shot past Alisson Becker, Liverpool's goalkeeper.
Liverpool play against Naples in the semi-finals of the Premier League at 15:00 on Wednesday at the San Paolo stadium in Naples, Italy.
Chelsea play against Videoton in UEFA's Europa League at 15:00 on Thursday in London.
The number of dead rises to 832 after a tsunami in Indonesia
The number of deaths caused by the earthquake and tsunami in Indonesia has risen to 832, according to a report from the country's disaster agency in the early hours of Sunday.
Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, the agency's spokeswoman, said at a press conference that a number of people had been buried underneath when buildings collapsed during the earthquake that occurred on Friday and was 7.5 on the Richter scale. The earthquake caused tsunamas that reached up to 6 metres high.
The city of Palu, which has over 380,000 inhabitants, was covered with debris from buildings that had collapsed.
Police arrested a 32-year-old man on suspicion of murder after a woman was stabbed to death
A murder investigation has been initiated after a woman's body was found in Birkenhead, Merseyside this morning.
A 44-year-old woman with stab wounds was found at 7:55 this morning near Grayson Mews on John Street and a 32-year-old man was arrested on suspicion of murder.
Police have urged people in the area to come forward if they saw or heard anything.
Investigator Brian O’Hagan said: “The investigation has just begun but I would like to ask all those who were near John Street in Birkenhead to contact us if they saw or heard anything suspicious.”
I would also like to ask everyone, especially taxi drivers, who may have caught something in a photo with their car cameras to contact us, because they could have important information for our investigation.”
A police spokeswoman has confirmed that the woman who died lived in Birkenhead and that she was found inside an apartment.
In the afternoon, people who thought they knew the woman had come to ask where she was found this morning.
The investigation continues and police say they are working to inform the victim's closest relatives.
A taxi driver, who lives in Grayson Mews, tried to get back to his apartment but police told him that no one was allowed to enter the building or leave it.
He was speechless when he discovered what had happened.
Residents have now been told that many hours could pass until they are allowed back in.
A police officer was heard telling a man that the entire area is now considered to be the scene of a crime.
Crying woman came to the scene.
She keeps repeating, "It's so terrible."
At 14:00 there were two police cars inside a marked area of the police and one car just outside it.
Numerous police officers stood inside the marked area and monitored the residential buildings.
Anyone who might have any information is asked to send a direct message to @MerPolCC, call 101 or contact the Crime Line anonymously at 0800 555 111 and provide the number 247 from September 30.
