A pig attacks a Chinese farmer and kills him
A pig attacked a Chinese farmer and killed him at a market in southwestern China, according to local news reports.
The man, nicknamed only by his surname "Yuan," was found dead with a broken artery and covered in blood near a pen on the market in Liupanshui in Guizhou Province, according to news reports from the South China Morning Post on Sunday.
A pig farmer prepares to inject a pig vaccine into a porcine pen on May 30, 2005 in Xining, Qinghai province, China.
According to reports, he had traveled with his uncle from neighboring Yunnan on Wednesday to sell 15 pigs on the market.
The next morning, the uncle found him dead and saw that a door into a nearby porridge stood open.
He said the inside of the pen had been a big boar with a bloody mouth.
A forensic examination confirmed that the 250 kg boar had killed the farmer, according to the report.
"My uncle's feet were bloodied and wounded," the uncle said, with the last name "Wu," to the Guiyang Evening News news agency.
A security camera footage showed Yuan arriving on the market at 4:40 in the morning to feed his pigs.
His body was found about an hour later.
The animal that killed the man was not owned by Yuan or his uncle.
The market owner told Evening News that the pig had been locked inside so it didn't attack anyone else while police gathered evidence at the scene.
According to news reports, Yuan's family and the market's owners are in talks for compensation for his death.
There are documented instances of pigs attacking people, although this is rare.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband on their farm in Massachusetts and seriously wounded the man.
Ten years earlier, a 300-kg pig clamped a Welsh farmer on a tractor until his wife drove the animal away.
After a farmer from Oregon was eaten by his pigs in 2012, another farmer from Manitoba told CBC News that pigs are usually not aggressive, but that blood flavors can provoke aggression among them.
"They're just playing.
They're curious, they don't want to hurt anyone.
You just have to treat them with respect," he said.
The remnants of Hurricane Rosa carry heavy precipitation over the southwestern part of the United States
As predicted, the concentration of Hurricane Rosa decreases as it crosses cooler oceans on the north coast of Mexico.
However, Rosa will carry heavy precipitation that could cause flooding in northern Mexico and southwestern United States in the coming days.
Rósa became a hurricane in category 1, with a wind strength of 136 km per hour, at 5 o'clock in the morning until the east coast on Sunday, and it was located just over 600 km southwest of Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
Rosa is scheduled to head north on Sunday.
Simultaneously, there is a depression in formation over the Pacific Ocean and it will move east towards the west coast of the United States. When Rosa arrives at the Baja California peninsula on Monday as a tropical storm, it will follow a high tropical drift in a northerly direction into the southwestern United States.
Rósa will follow up to 25 cm of precipitation in some parts of Mexico on Monday.
Then the tropical debris will mix with the depression approachinging to generate heavy and extensive precipitation over the southwest in the coming days.
Local 2.5 to 10 cm precipitation can cause dangerous sudden floods, movement of the soil and possible landslides in the desert.
Excessive tropical humidity will cause up to 5 to 10 cm of precipitation per hour in some places, especially in southern Nevada and Arizona.
Up to 5 to 10 cm of precipitation is expected in some parts of the southwest side, especially in most of the state of Arizona.
There is the possibility of sudden flooding with rapidly deteriorating conditions due to tropical rains.
People are strongly advised against going on foot into the desert when there is a risk of tropical rain.
Heavy raining can turn ravines into belly rivers and thunderstorms result in strong winds and dusty storms.
The approaching depression will carry local rainfall in certain areas of southern California's shoreline.
There is a possibility of more than 1 cm of precipitation which could cause slight movement of the soil and ice on the roads.
This is the area's first precipitation during the rainy season.
Several scattered tropical sheds will start approaching Arizona late on Sunday and early Monday, before rainfall spread over a larger area late Monday and Tuesday.
Heavy precipitation will spread across four states on Tuesday and last through Wednesday.
High temperature changes are expected in October throughout the United States as the Arctic is cooling, but the tropics remain relatively warm.
Occasionally this can cause extreme temperature fluctuations over short distances.
There was a good example of a huge temperature difference in the United States on Sunday.
There is a nearly 7-degree temperature difference between Kansas City, Missouri and Omaha, Nebraska, and between St. Louis and Des Moines in Iowa.
Over the next few days, persistent summers will try to build up and spread out again.
A large part of the central and eastern U.S. is expected to get a warm start in October with temperatures around 25 degrees or more from the plains in the south to the northeastern states.
Temperatures could go up to 25 degrees in New York City on Tuesday which is about 6 degrees higher than the average.
Our long-term weather forecast indicates that there is a high probability of an average temperature for the eastern part of the United States in the first half of October.
More than 20 million people were watching interrogations of Brett Kavanaugh
More than 20 million people watched the exciting testimony of Brett Kavanaugh, the nomined Supreme Court justice, on Tuesday and the testimony of Christine Blasey Ford, the woman who accused him of sexual miscarriages that was supposed to have occurred in the 1990s. The testimony was sent out on six different channels.
At the same time, the political standoff continued and television stations paused the regular schedule to publish the latest news on Friday: Jeff Flake, a senator from Arizona, filed a bill so that the FBI could investigate the allegations for one week.
Ford told the Senate Judiciary Committee she was confident that Kavanaug had groped her and tried to undress her at a school party.
Kavanaugh said in an emotional testimonial that he was confident it hadn't happened.
It's likely that more people have watched the trial than Nielsen's 20.4 million people said had watched Friday.
The company measured the average views of the television channels CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
Numbers weren't available immediately for other TV channels that showed the interrogations, including PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network.
Nielsen also usually has trouble measuring people's views in offices.
To put this into perspective, this spectator group is similar in size as for the final in football or the Academy Awards.
According to Nielsen, most of the interrogations, which took place all daily, on the Fox News Channel or 5.69 million.
ABC was second with 3.26 million spectators.
CBS had 3.1 million, NBC had 2.94 million, MSNBC had 2.89 million and CNN had 2.52 million, according to Nielsen.
Interest remained great after the trial.
Flake was the centerpiece of Friday's overall game.
After the Republican Party's office released an announcement that he would elect Kavanaugh in favour, CNN's and CSP's cameramakers captured pictures of when protesters shouted at him on Friday morning as he tried to go into an elevator to get to a meeting of the Judicial Committee.
He lasted for a few minutes while it rained heavily over him on live television with CNN.
One woman said, "I'm standing right in front of you."
“Do you think he’s telling the countrymen the truth?”
People said to him, “You have power while so many women stand powerless.”
Flake said his office had issued a statement, and before the elevator doors closed, he said he would comment further at the committee's meeting.
Cable and television stations all had a live broadcast later when the judiciary committee voted on whether to forward Kavanaugh's nominee to the Senate election.
Flake said he'd only do it if the FBI investigated the allegations against the nominees next week, but a minority of Democrats have urged it.
Flake was partly convinced after conversations with his friend, Senator Chris Coons.
