Volcano spouts ashes over Mexico City
Ash from the volcano Popocatepetl has reached the southern capital of Mexico.
The National Center for Disaster Prevention (U.S.) advised Mexicans on Saturday to stay away from the volcano after activity increased in the crater, and 183 cases of gas and ash emissions were recorded over a 24-hour period.
The center monitored vibrations and tremors from the volcano.
Pictures on social media showed a thin layer of ashes on car windows in Mexico City's neighborhoods, such as in Xochimilco.
Geophysicists have noticed increased activity in the volcano, 72 kilometers southeast of the capital, since an earthquake of 7.1 on Richter struck central Mexico in September 2017.
"Don Goyo" has been active since 1994.
Police in clashes with Catalan separatists just before independence referency
Six people were arrested in Barcelona on Saturday after independent protesters confronted riot police and thousands took part in demonstrations on the occasion of Catalonia's first birthday of the controversial election to declare independence.
A group of masked separatists stoned riot police with eggs and threw a powder paint that formed dark dust clouds on the streets, which would usually otherwise be full of tourists.
Conflict also broke out later in the day and police deployed clubs to keep fighting in check.
Separatist groups shouted, “We don’t forget and we won’t forgive” for a few hours while their opponents shouted: “Long live Spain.”
Fourteen needed treatment due to minor injuries they suffered during the protests, according to local media reports.
There is great excitement in the independence zone one year after the referendum on October 1 was declared illegal by authorities in Madrid, but was welcomed by Catalan separatists.
A majority of voters voted for independence but participation was poor since most of those who opposed the separation ignored the election.
According to Catalan authorities, nearly 1000 people were injured last year when police tried to stop the elections at polling stations across the area, but it had conflicts.
Groups of independents had stayed outside overnight on Friday to prevent protests in support of the country's police.
Protesting took place but was forced to take another route.
Narcis Termes, a 68-year-old electrician, took part in separatist protests with his wife, saying he was no longer hopeful that Catalonia would gain independence.
"Last year we experienced some of our best moments.
"I saw my parents crying with joy for being able to vote, but now we're stuck," he said.
Despite having achieved a necessary but scarce victory in last December's regional elections, Catalonia's independent parties have struggled to stay strong this year, as many of their best-known leaders are either in voluntary exile or in detention pending trials because of their role in the organisation of the vote and the Declaration of Independence.
Joan Puig, a 42-year-old mechanic who took the protests in support of the police on his phone, said the politicians of both factions had urged the dispute.
"Excitement is increasing all the time," he said.
On Saturday, Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders who have served time in prison and awaited trial since late last year, announced that he would run for the European Parliament next year.
"My candidacy in the European elections is the best way to condemn the regression in the field of democratic values and the oppression we've seen the Spanish government applying," he said.
