Hurricane Rosa threatens Phoenix, Las Vegas, Salt Lake City with sudden floods (drought areas could benefit)
It's rare that a tropical depression spreads over Arizona, but it's likely to happen early next week when the remnants of Hurricane Rosa are spreading over the southwest, with the risk of sudden flooding.
The country's Meteorological Office has already issued warnings about sudden floods for Monday and Tuesday in western Arizona and the south and west of Nevada, southeastern California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to take a direct route over Phoenix on Tuesday and arrive late Monday with precipitation.
Phoenix's Met Office tweeted that only "ten hurricanes have managed to maintain strength to be classified as tropical storms or troughs less than 320 km from Phoenix since 1950!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane less than 64 km from the Arizona border.”
Last forecasts from the country's hurricane center predict 5-10 cm precipitation, with single precipitation up to 15 cm in Mogollon Rim in Arizona.
Precipitation is likely to be 2,5 to 5 cm in other areas in the southwest, including the central part of the Rocky Mountains and The Great Basin, with the possibility of individual precipitation of up to 10 cm.
For those who are not at risk of sudden flooding, precipitation from Rosa could be a blessing because there are great droughts in the area.
Although floods can cause a lot of damage, some of this precipitation will have a positive effect, as drought is now in the southwest.
According to U.S. drought control, at least an extreme amount of drought in over 40 percent of Arizona, but that's the second-highest category," according to weather.com reports.
Hurricane Rosa will first land on Mexico's Baja California peninsula.
Rosa was still classified as a hurricane on Sunday morning with a maximum wind strength of 136 km per hour and 620 km south of Punta Eugenia in Mexico and travelling in a northerly direction at 19 km/h.
The storm crosses the cooler seas in the Pacific Ocean and its power is therefore declining.
It is therefore expected to land in Mexico as a tropical storm on Monday afternoon or Monday night.
There is the possibility of heavy precipitation in some parts of Mexico, with a significant risk of flooding.
“Precipitation of 7.5 to 15 cm is expected in Baja California and into the northwest of Sonora, with the possibility of up to 25 cm precipitation,” weather.com said in a statement.
Rosa will then head north over Mexico, as a tropical storm, and then reach Arizona's border early Tuesday morning as a tropical depression. She will then continue up through Arizona and arrive at southern Utah late Tuesday night.
“The main danger expected from Rosa or its remnants is extreme precipitation in Baja California, northwestern Sonora and in the southwestern part of the U.S. desert area,” the country’s hurricane center said.
These precipitations are expected to cause life-threatening sudden flooding and movement of soil in the deserts, and landslides in the mountains.
