Hundred people were killed after an earthquake and tsunami in Indonesia, and the number of dead is likely to rise
At least 384 people were killed and many were washed away as huge waves hit beaches, when a massive earthquake and tsunami struck the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, according to local authorities on Saturday.
Thousands of people had gathered at a festival on the beach in the city of Palu on Friday when up to six-metre-high waves hit the beach at sunset, flushed crowds into the ocean and destroyed everything that happened to them.
The tsunami was formed after an earthquake that was 7.5° at Richter.
"When the tidal turbulence formed yesterday, people were still on the beach and didn't escape immediately, so they were killed," Sutopo Purwo Nugroho spokeswoman for Indonesia's catastrophic organisation said at an information meeting in Jakarta.
"The tsunami didn't come alone, she brought with her cars, tree trunks, houses, it hit everything that was on land," Nugroho said, adding that the tsunami had travelled across an open ocean at 800 kilometres before it hit the beach.
Some climbed a tree to escape the tsunami and survived.
About 16,700 people were transferred to 24 centers in Palu.
Photographs from the air, published by the Catastrophic Institute, revealed many destroyed buildings and shops, twisted and destroyed bridges and mosques surrounded by water.
Aftershocks continued riding across the coastal city on Saturday.
A series of earthquakes occurred in an area with 2.4 million inhabitants.
Indonesia's agency on food and technology use said in a statement that the energy released during Friday's major quake had been about 200 times greater than from the atomic bomb that fell on the city of Hiroshima during World War II.
The geographical location of the city, which lies at the end of a long and narrow bay, could have increased the size of the tidal wave, the agency said.
Nugroho said the damage was "significant" and that thousands of houses, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels had collapsed.
The bodies of several victims were found trapped under debris from collapsed buildings, he said, adding that 540 people had been injured and 29 were missing.
Nugroho said the casualties and property damage could be greater along the shoreline 300 km north from Palu in an area known as Donggala which is closer to the source of the earthquake.
Communicating “lies completely down and no information is received” from Donggala, Nugroho said.
"Over 300,000 people live there," the Red Cross said in a statement, adding that their staff and volunteers were on their way to the affected areas.
"It's already a tragedy, but it could get much worse," the Red Cross said.
The agency was criticized by many on Saturday for failing to report that a tsunami had flooded Palu, although public officials said the waves had arrived within the time the warning was issued.
In a video posted on social media, one could hear people shouting from the upper floor of a building to warn the people on the street below that a tidal wave was approaching.
Just a few minutes later, the tsunami hits the beach and carries off buildings and cars.
Reuters couldn't confirm the video right away.
The earthquake and tsunami caused a power outage that hindered communication about Palu and made it difficult for authorities to organize relief efforts.
The army has begun deploying planes with auxiliary supplies from Jakarta and other cities, according to authorities, but people who have been helped by the region still desperately need food and other necessities.
The city's airport has only been reopened for relief operations and will be closed until October.
President Joko Widodo plans to visit refugee centers in Palu on Sunday.
The number of deaths exceeds 800 after a tsunami in Indonesia.
The situation is very bad.
The staff of World Vision from Donggala has reached the city of Palu, where it seeks shelter in tent cities that were set up in the park outside their offices.
"People said they've seen a lot of houses completely destroyed," he said.
The situation is very bad.
Relief groups have started difficult relief efforts, but some have complained that foreign aid workers with extensive expertise would be hampered from traveling to Pala.
According to Indonesian regulations, foreign financing, supplies and staff may only begin to arrive in the event of a declared disaster zone.
It hasn't been done yet.
"It's still a local casualty zone," Aulia Arriani, a spokeswoman for the Indonesian Red Cross, said.
“Once the government has declared that it is a catastrophic area, we can open up foreign aid, but there has been no declaration yet.”
When the second night fell over Palu after the earthquake and tsunami on Friday, friends and their families, who are still missing, held out the hope that their loved ones would become one of the miraculous sagas that cast hopefully on the depressing headlines accompanying natural disasters.
On Saturday, a little boy was rescued.
On Sunday, aid workers rescued a woman who had been trapped under the rubble for two days with her mother's corpse.
Gendon Subandono, the coach of the Indonesian glider team, had been the coach of two gliders, who are now missing, for the Asian Games which ended earlier this month in Indonesia.
Others trapped inside the Roa Roa hotel were his students, including Mr. Mandagi.
"As a man with a long experience in gliding, I've got my own emotional burden," he said.
Mr. Gendon reported that the hours after news broke within the glider group that the Roa Roa hotel had collapsed, he had sent WhatsApp messages desperately to the contestants who stayed in Palu and took part in the beach festival.
His message, however, showed only a gray signal, instead of blue.
"I think that means the message wasn't received," he said.
