Macedonians voted in referendum on whether to change the country's name
Macedonia's citizens voted in a referendum on Sunday on whether the country's name should be changed to "Republic of North Macedonia" in an attempt to resolve a decades-long disagreement with Greece, which has blocked their application for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece has a province named Macedonia and claims that the name of their neighbors in the north is equivalent to a requirement in their territory and therefore denied them entry into NATO and the EU.
The two governments reached an agreement in June that was based on the new name, but nationalist opponents argue that the change will undermine the identity of the majority of Macedonia's population of Slavic origin.
Gjorge Ivanov has said he will not take part in the referendum and a campaign to ignore it has raised doubts that participation will reach the 50 percent minimum required for the vote to be considered valid.
"Do you want to join NATO and the EU by accepting the agreement with Greece?"
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, say this is a fair fee for Macedonia to gain membership of alliances such as the EU and NATO, but the country is one of those formed after the fall of Yugoslavia.
"I have come today to vote on the country's future for the young people of Macedonia so that they can live freely under the protection of the European Union because it means a safer life for all of us," Olivera Georgijevska, 79, said in Skopje.
Although the result is not legally binding, enough MPs have said they will abide by it and tie it into law.
The name change would require the approval of two-thirds of Congress.
The Elections Committee said there had been no announcement of anything unusual at 1 o'clock in the afternoon.
However, participation was just 16 per cent, compared with 34 per cent participating in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 percent of registered voters voted.
"I came to vote because of my children, our place is in Europe," said Gjose Tanevski, a 62-year-old voter in the capital Skopje.
Macedonia's Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and their son Dushko voted in Macedonia's referendum on whether to change the country's name, but it would facilitate its way into NATO and the European Union, in Strumica, Macedonia on 30 September 2018.
In front of parliament in Skopje, 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov worked to prepare a small stage and line up chairs in front of tents that had been erected by those who will ignore the vote.
“We’re in favor of Nato and the EU, but we want to become members with our heads high, don’t go through the service door,” Kavardarkov said.
“We’re a poor country, but we’re built.
If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can turn to China or Russia and become part of the Eurasian Union.”
Prime Minister Zaev says membership of NATO will bring the necessary funding to Macedonia, where the proportion of the unemployed is over 20 percent.
"I believe the vast majority will agree that over 80 percent of the country's population is in favor of the EU and NATO," Zaev said after having voted.
He said approval would be "confirmation of our future."
An opinion poll that was published on Monday from Macedonia's policy research agency showed that between 30 and 43 percent of voters would participate in the referendum, but it's lower than the minimum required.
Another opinion poll from the TV channel Telma TV showed that 57 percent of respondents planned to vote on Sunday.
Of those, 70 percent said they're going to say yes.
In order for the referendum to be considered valid, attendance must be 50 percent plus one vote.
If the vote is not approved, it could mean the first serious setback for the government's policies, which are in favor of the West, since taking place in May last year.
See: Sergio Aguero, the Manchester City player, goes through all of Brighton's defensives and scores
Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling went lightly with Brighton's defence in Manchester City's victory 2-0 on Saturday at Etihad Stadium in Manchester.
Aguero made it look absurdibly easily when he scored his goal in the 65th minute.
The Argentine striker received a pass in the middle of the pitch at the start of the attack.
He sprinted past Brighton's three defenders and then went out onto the open pitch.
Aguero then became surrounded by four green-clad defenders.
He got past one defender and then ran away a few more just off Brighton's penalty area.
Then he sent to the left and found Sterling.
The English striker used the first touch within the penalty area to send the ball back to Aguero, who used his right foot to play Mathew Ryan, Brighton's goalkeeper, with a shot into the right side of the goal.
"Aguero has some foot problems," City manager Pep Guardiola told reporters.
"We talked about him playing 55, 60 minutes.
Then it happened.
"We were lucky he scored at that moment."
But it was Sterling who gave them the blue first goal in the Premier League game.
That goal came in 29 minutes.
Aguero conceded a pass far inside Brighton's field half in that attack.
He sent an impressive long ball on the left to Leroy Sane.
Sane kicked the ball a couple of times and then sent to Sterling towards the far post.
City's striker sent the ball into the net just before he slipped.
