Labour is no longer a tolerant church.
He doesn't tolerate those who express their minds
When Momentum's activists in my party voted to give me a rebuke, it was hardly a surprise.
After all, I'm the last of many Labour MPs who have been told we'ren't welcome, just for expressing our minds.
Joan ryan, my fellow congressman, received a similar treatment because she fiercely objected to anti-Jewish prejudice.
In my case, the proposal to give me rebukes came about because I disagreed with Jeremy Corbyn.
The issues were the importance of having a responsible economic policy, on national security, about Europe, ironically similar issues and Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
The announcement of the meeting for Nottingham East on Friday said that "we want the meetings to leave nothing out of control and to be effective."
In my eight-year career as a member of the Labour Party, Friday meetings have most often been just that.
Unfortunately, that’s not the tone of many today’s meetings, and the promise of “gentler and better” politics has been forgotten, if it ever started.
It has become increasingly clear that different opinions have not passed in the Labour Party and that all opinions are judged according to whether the party's leaders consider them acceptable.
This started shortly after Jeremy became a leader, when colleagues, who I previously thought had similar political views as me, began to expect me to take an absolute reversal and supported opinions that I had previously never agreed with, whether it was about national security or a common EU market area.
Every time I speak publicly - and then it doesn't matter what I'm saying - it's accompanied by crawling insults on social media calling for my expulsion, middle politics denied and told I shouldn't be part of the Labour Party.
And it's not just my experience.
I know I'm luckier than some of my colleagues, since most of the comments I make to myself are most often political.
I admire the professionalism and affirmation of those who have to endure the catastrophics of sexual or prejudiced insults on a daily basis but never retreat.
One of the biggest disappointments during this political era is how attacks and insults have become a normal part of everyday life.
Jeremy Corbyn said last week that Labour should nurture a tolerant culture.
The reality is that we are no longer a tolerant church and with every distrust proposal or amendment of the selection rules, the party becomes increasingly narrow.
I've been given a lot of advice over the last two years to keep my head down, don't get too high, and then I'd be "okay".
But I didn't decide to get involved in politics to do that.
Since I became a member of the Labour Party as a student 32 years ago, motivated by the negligence of Thatcher's government that left my classroom in disarray, I've campaigned for better public services for those who need it the most, whether I was in the city council or in parliament.
I've never hidden my political views, neither at the last election.
None in Nottingham East can be in any doubt about my policy views and where I disagree with the current leaders.
To those who supported the proposal on Friday, I would only want to say that when the country is heading for Brexit, which will harm homes, businesses and our public services, I don't understand the desire to spend time and energy on my loyalty to Labour's leadership.
But my only message that I want to send is not to Nottingham Momentum, but to those within my constituency, whether they are members of the Labour Party or not: I serve you with pride, and I promise that no threats of anomaly or political expulsion will discourage me from doing what I consider to be for the best interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie is Nottingham East Member of Parliament
Ayr 38 - 17 Melrose: Undefeated team Ayr goes to the top
Two late touchdowns may have skewed the final result something, but there's no doubt Ayr deserved the win in this embarrassing match of the day in the Tennent's Premiership league.
They're now at the top of the list and the only team out of ten that's still undefeated.
Ultimately, it was their stronger defense and better use of opportunities that made the locals victorious and coach Peter Murchie had every right to be satisfied.
"We've got tough opposition in our games so far and we're still undefeated, so I have to be happy," he said.
Robyn Christie of Melrose said: 'Ayr deserves praise, they took advantage of their opportunities better than us.'
Mark Grant Anderson brought Ayr over in the 14th minute after Frazier Climo turned the offense around, but a yellow card on the Scottish captain, who was allowed to play the game with the Warriors, allowed Melrose to let the numbers speak and Jason Baggot managed to score a touchdown.
Climo increased Ayr's lead with a penalty before he scored with a kick just before half-time and then a touchdown and Ayr came over 17-5 at half-time.
But Melrose started the second half by force and Patrick Anderson's touchdown, after Baggot turned the offense around, reduced the goal difference to five points.
A long play-out was then required to nurture a serious injury at Ruaridh Knott, who had to be carried off the playing field, and after that, Ayr still increased his lead with a touchdown from Stafford McDowall, after Climo turned the offense around.
Blair Macpherson, Ayr's captain, then received the yellow card and Melrose again took advantage of being a man over with a touchdown from Bruce Colvine, at the end of a tough offense.
However, the locals came back strong and when Struan Hutchinson received the yellow card for having tackled Climo without the ball, MacPherson scored a touchdown behind Ayr's offense, after a free kick.
Climo turned the offense around, as he did again immediately after the restart, after Kyle Rowe's kick David Amrstrong from the penalty area and sent to Gregor Henry who scored the team's fifth touchdown.
Star Still Game episodes ready for a new career in the restaurant industry
Ford Kieran, the star of the Still Game series, appears ready to move to restaurant operations after finding out that he was appointed manager of a restaurant company.
Kieran, 56, plays Jack Jarvis in the popular BBC television series, which he writes and plays in alongside his old comrades from the entertainment industry, Creg Hemphill.
The duo has announced that the upcoming ninth season will be the last and it appears that Kiernan has plans for Craiglang's life.
According to the official records, he is chief executive of Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor didn't want to say anything about the story, but the Scottish Sun's source suggested Kiernan wanted to become a participant in Glasgow's "living restaurant industry".
“The ocean is ours”: Bolivia’s stranded country hopes that a court will reopen its path to the Pacific Ocean
Sailors uphold the navy's headquarters in La Paz.
General buildings flag blue flag.
Naval stations from Lake Titicaca to the Amazon River are labeled slogan: “We have a right to the sea.
“It’s our duty to restore it.”
Across all the other landlocked the Bolivia's memory of the coastline, which was lost to Chile in a bloody 19th-century conflict, still in fresh memory - along with the desire to sail around the Pacific Ocean again.
These hopes are perhaps the strongest for decades, now that Bolivia is awaiting a ruling from the International Court of Justice in The Hague on October 1 after a five-year-long case.
"Bolivia possesses power, the unifying spirit and the serenity, and of course waits for the verdict with a positive mind," Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat, said.
Many residents of Bolivia will observe the ruling of the International Court of Justice on large screens throughout the country, in the hope that the court in The Hague will rule in favour of Bolivia's demand that Chile be obliged to grant Bolivia access to the sea, after many decades of difficult conversations.
Monday's verdict is also extremely important for Evo Morales, the country's Indonesian president who will face a controversial fight for next year's re-election.
"We're very close to returning to the Pacific," he promised in late August.
But some analysts believe it's unlikely that the court's ruling in Bolivia's favour, and that it'd make little difference if he did.
The court, which is headquartered in the Netherlands, has no power to give land from Chile and has stipulated that the ruling will not affect the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that the court's ruling comes only six months after listening to the closing arguments indicates that the case "hasn't been complicated," said Paz Sárate, an Icelandic expert on international law.
It is also possible that for the past four years Bolivia's case has not been brought to the fore, but has caused the country to return.
"Bolivia's current regime has stolen the issue of access to the sea," Zárate said.
Morale's military rhetoric has diminished all goodwill from Chile, she counted.
Bolivia and Chile will continue negotiations someday, but it will be hugely difficult after this.
The two countries have not exchanged diplomats since 1962.
Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, former president of Bolivia and the country's representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court's decision had taken an unusually short period.
On Monday, Bolivia will gain “a unique opportunity to open up a new relationship with Chile” and an opportunity to “end a disagreement dating back 139 years, for the benefit of both countries.”
Calzadilla also denied that Morales, who is still one of the most popular presidents in Latin America, was using this issue as a political crutch.
"Bolivia will never give up its right to have access to the Pacific," he added.
"The verdict is an opportunity to see that we need to recover from the past."
North Korea says there won't be any nuclear disarmament unless it can trust the United States
Ri Yong Ho, North Korea's foreign minister, says the country will never take down its nuclear weapons first if it can't trust Washington.
Ri gave his speech on Saturday at the United Nations General Assembly.
He called on the United States to follow up on the promises they made at a summit in Singapore between the leaders of the nations.
His remarks come now that US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears to be about to resume talks on nuclear weapons, more than three months after the meeting in Singapore with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un.
Ri says it's "dreamers" to think that the U.S.'s continued trade embargos and protests against the end of the Korean War will ever crush North Korea.
Washington doesn't want to accept the statement unless Pyongyang first takes clear action to take down his nuclear weapons.
Both Kim and US President Donald Trump want to meet again at another meeting.
However, there are strong doubts that Pyongyang is serious about taking down weapons that the country probably sees as the only way to ensure its safety.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare for the next meeting between Kim and Trump.
Paris fashion shows present the latest collection of large headgears that will arrive in fashion stores near you
If you want to enlarge your hat collection or be completely hidden from the sun, look no further.
Designers Valentino and Thom Browne showcased a variety of over-sized hats for their SS19 line-up in the fashion scene that fascinated the jet crew at Paris Fashion Week.
A number of unsuitable images have been posted on Instagram this summer and these designers have had their innovative creations on display at fashion shows.
Valentino's main work was a large light brown hat with wide-breased bears that resembled feathers and the hidden heads of the models.
Of other oversized accessories, watermelons could be seen set of precious stones, sorcerer's hat and even pineapple, but these items are not designed to keep warm on the head.
Thom Browne also introduced a variety of strange masks, precisely at the right time for Halloween.
Many of the colorful masks had stitched lips and resembled Hannibal Lecter rather than high fashion.
One of the masks resembled diving equipment with a breathing tube and glasses, while another resembled melting ice in bread form.
If you're interested in fashion, you're lucky.
Fashion experts speculate that the big hats could soon come on sale in boutiques near you.
Big hats come close on the heels of “La Bomba”, a straw hat with over 60cm large bars that has adorned the heads from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The fashion brand behind the extremely unsuitable hat, which was seen on all social media platforms, was the inspiration for another large accessory that appeared on the fashion stage – a straw beach bag that was almost as big as the swimsuit-clad model that carried it.
The orange bus, with strawbards and a white handle made of leather, was the main part of the La Riviera SS10 clothing line Jacquemus at Paris Fashion Week.
Luke Armitage, stylist of celebrities, told FEMAIL: "I expect to see big hats and beachcases appearing in fashion stores for next summer - since the designer has caused such a huge impact, it should be difficult to ignorate the demand for oversized accessories."
John Edward: Language skills are a must for cosmopolitans
Scotland's private schools are known for the outstanding achievements of their students and it has continued in 2018 with new, impressive test results, which are still reinforced by individual and group achievements in sports, arts, music and other community workshops.
These schools have up to 30,000 students all over Scotland, with The Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) in charge of them, and they strive to provide their students and their parents with the best possible services.
Private schools aim to prepare their students for further postgraduate studies, the career they have chosen and their status as cosmopolitans.
As a teaching sector that can assemble and teach according to specially designed curricula, we see that new languages continue to be a popular curriculum in the schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If you talk to a man in a language he understands, it touches his mind.
“If you speak to him in his native language, it touches his heart.”
This is an important reminder that we can't just rely on English to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
This year's exam results show us that languages are at the top of the lists with the highest proportions of students who pass exams in private schools.
68 percent of students who learned a foreign language received a decent grade of A.
The data collected from 74 schools within SCIS showed that 72 percent of students received a decent grade of A in mandarin, while 72 percent of those who learned German, 69 percent of those who learned French, and 63 percent of those who learned Spanish also received a grade of A.
This shows that private schools in Scotland support foreign languages as the necessary competencies that children and young people will surely need in the future.
At the moment, languages, as an elective subject, are placed in the same rankings as science-related study material (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) in the curriculators of private schools and elsewhere.
An opinion poll conducted in 2014 by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills found that due to the reasons that employers indicated that it was difficult to hire jobs, there was 17 per cent due to a shortage of people with language skills.
That's why language proficiency is becoming increasingly important to preparing young people for their future jobs.
Now that more and more jobs require language proficiency, these skills are essential in a globalized world.
No matter what career people choose, they will have a real advantage in the future if they learn a different language, but it is a skill that lasts a lifetime.
The ability to directly communicate with people from other countries will give a multilingual character an automatic advantage to the competitors.
According to an opinion poll conducted by YouGov on over 4000 adult British in 2013, 75 percent of them couldn't speak another language well enough to sustain dialogue, and French was the only language that was spoken by a two-digit percentage, or 15 percent.
That's why it's important for today's children to invest immediately in language teaching.
Multilingualism, especially in emerging market countries, will give children a better opportunity to find a meaningful job.
In Scotland there is a difference between the schools of what language they teach.
A certain number of schools will focus on more classical new languages, while others will teach languages that are considered to be most important to the UK when looking until 2020, such as mandarin or Japanese.
No matter where your child's interest lies, they will always be able to choose from languages in private schools with specially educated teachers.
Scottish private schools undertake to provide a learning environment, which will prepare children and equip them with the skills they need to succeed, no matter what the future entails.
It cannot be denied that during this time, in the global business environment, language continues to be necessary for the country's future, and it must be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should really be seen as “international knowledge of communication.”
Private schools will continue to offer selection, diversity and dominance for Scotland's youngsters.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edwards is the chairman of the Scottish Private School Board
LeBron plays his first game for the Lakers in San Diego on Sunday
The wait is coming to an end for fans who want to see LeBron James play their first game for the Los Angeles Lakers.
Lakers coach Luke Walton has announced that James will play on Sunday in the opening game of the season against the Denver Nuggets in San Diego.
It has not yet been decided in how many minutes he will play.
"It's going to be for more than a minute and shorter than 48," Walton said on Laker's official website.
Mike Trudell, a reporter for the Lakers, tweeted that James would probably play for a limited time.
After training earlier this week, James was questioned about his plans for the Lakers' six-game schedule for the start of the season.
"During this period of my career, I don't have to play games before the season to prepare," he said.
Timings for Trump's Election Session in West Virginia and YouTube channel
President Donald Trump will hold a number of general election meetings and the first is tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
He's one of five scheduled meetings next week, where he'll also stop at friendly venues, including in Tennessee and Mississippi.
Now that the vote to confirm his selection of a new Supreme Court justice has been put on hold, Trump aims to build up support for the next midterm elections, because Republicans are at risk of losing control of Congress when votes will be cast in November.
What time is Trump's campaign in West Virginia, and how can you watch it online?
Trump's meeting in Wheeling in West Virginia is due to begin at 19.00 East Coastal Time this evening, Saturday, September 29, 2018.
You can watch the meeting with Trump in West Virginia online below via a live broadcast on YouTube.
It is likely that Trump will discuss the trial of Brett Kavanaugh, the nomined Supreme Court justice, where there was tensions over allegations of sexual harassment. The Senate vote to confirm him as a judge was put on hold for up to a week while the FBI investigates the allegations.
But Trump's main goal is to help the Republicans recover better, but they are awaiting a tough election in November.
According to Trump's election campaign, the five meetings that will be held next week are aimed at "fulfilling volunteers and supporter enthusiasm as Republicans are trying to protect and increase the majority they have in the Senate and in the Senate," according to the Reuters news agency.
"Controlling is so necessary for the president's plan that he will travel to as many states as it is possible now that a busy campaign period begins," an unnamed spokesman from Trump's election campaign told Reuters.
Tonight's meeting will be held at Wesbanco Stadium in Wheeling and supporters are expected to come from "Ohio and Pennsylvania and that the media from Pittsburgh will report it," according to the West Virginia Metro News.
Saturday's meeting will be Trump's second visit to West Virginia this month, but he prevailed with over 40 percent difference in this state in 2016.
Trump wants to try to assist Patrick Morrissey, a Republican candidate in West Virginia to the Senate, but he's at the bottom of the polls.
"It's not a good idea for Morrissey that the president needs to come and try to increase his popularity," said Simon Haeder, a political scientist at the University of West Virginia, according to a Reuters news agency.
Ryder Trophy 2018: U.S
After three unilateral rounds, the four-game, which was Saturday afternoon, could be exactly what was needed in this Ryder Cup competition.
Swing in success is a sports concept that has been invented and that players certainly believe in, and never more than in competitions like this.
Where would they say that success lay now?
"They had a six-point advantage and now it's four points, so the success lies rather on our side right now, I suppose," Jordan Spieth said.
Europe, of course, has an advantage, four points ahead with an additional twelve at stake.
The Americans, as Spieth said, believe they have a bit of wind in their sails and they can be hopeful over many things, not least the form by Spieth and Justin Thomas who played together all day and each got three points out of four.
Spieth has played very well all the way to the putting green and leads by his example.
The cheers rose as the round progressed and he put down an important putt to shepherd all four points in the game after he and Thomas had been two points under after two games.
His putt, who secured them victory in the game on the 15th hole, received a similar shout of joy, a shout of the kind that tells a man that he believes the U.S. team still has the hope of victory.
"You just have to dive deep and take care of your own game," Spieth said.
That's the only thing these players can do right now.
18 holes to earn points.
The only players with more points than Spieth and Thomas in the last two days are Francesco Molinaro and Tommy Fleetwooof, the indisputable Ryder trophy.
This strange but breathtaking European pair have won four games out of four and can't do anything wrong.
"Moliwood" were the only pair that got a bogey on Saturday afternoon, but they also managed to avoid a bogey on a Saturday morning, on Friday afternoon and on a nine-hole course on Friday morning.
That game and how their energy seems to flow both to and from the noisy spectators clearly shows that they are the pair who have to win on Sunday, and there would be no more popular player to seal the possible victory of the European team than Fleetwood or Molinari, as the sun sets over the Le Golf National stadium.
The best option would be that both do it simultaneously at different holes.
However, it's still too early to talk about the European team's victory.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson took Sergio Garcia, the hero of the quadruple in the morning, where he played with Alex Noren.
Skolli and two doubles on the first nine holes buried the Spaniard and the Swede into a hole that they never managed to scratch their way out of.
But on Sunday, nobody can help you out of your hole.
The quadruples and the four games are so exciting to watch at close due to the communication between the couples, their advice, the advice they don't give and how the game schedule can change in one go.
Europe has played better as a team so far and has a considerable advantage for the final day, but this quarter-final also showed that the U.S
Europe with a 10-6 lead for the final day of the Ryder Cup
Europe will have a good advantage for the final day of the Ryder Cup after Saturday's quadruple and quadruple, with an advantage of 10-6 against the United States.
The star duo Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari led the attack with two victories over Tiger Woods, who struggled, and gained four points on the Le Golf National course.
Thomas Bjorn's European team, who would like to regain the medal trophy they lost on the Hazeltine field two years ago, showed great dominance over the U.S. team in the morning's quadruple game and won 3-1.
The U.S. showed more resistance in the quadruples and won two games but failed to work up the scoring margin.
Jim Furyk's team needed eight points from 12 individual games on Sunday to continue the cup.
Fleetwood is the first European rookie to win four points in a row and he and Molinari, who received the nickname "Molliwood" after a phenomenal weekend, are only the second pair who won four points from their first four games in the history of the Ryder Cup.
After defeating Woods and Patrick Reed in the quadruple game, they showed great team spirit and beat Woods and American rookier Bryson Dechambeau 5-4.
Woods, who withdrew himself through two games on Saturday, showed his genius once in a while, but he has now lost 19 of his 29 games in four-ball and four-games, including seven games in a row.
Justin Rose, who was rested for this morning's quadruple, came back to his partner, Henriks Stenson in the quadruples, winning 2-1 against Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka - who are in first and third place on the list of the world's best golfers.
However, Europe didn't do everything well during this mild and gusty day southwest of Paris.
A three-time winner in big golf competitions, Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas set their sights for Americans by two points on Saturday.
They won with difficulty 2-1 against Jon Rahm from Spain and Ian Poulter in four-pouch matches and later came back and defeated Poulter and Rory Mcllroy 4-3 in the four-matches after losing the first two holes.
It has only happened twice in Ryder's history that a team won a four-point margin at the start of an individual competition, even though Furyk's team only needed a draw to continue the trophy.
Having been the second best for two days, however, it appears that Sunday's counter-attack will overwhelm them.
North Korea says unilateral disarmament 'impossibly' without confidence
North Korea's foreign minister told the UN on Saturday that continued trade embargoes only increased their distrust of the United States and it would be impossible for the country to unilaterally take down their nuclear weapons unilaterally under such circumstances.
Ri Yong Ho told the annual U.N. General Assembly that North Korea had taken "significant measures to show goodwill" in the past year, such as stopping nuclear and missile tests, taking down the nuclear test area, and promising to stop the development of nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
"However, we haven't seen any corresponding reaction from the United States," he said.
“Without confidence in the United States, there is no certainty about the security of our country, and under such circumstances it is impossible for unilaterally to take down our nuclear weapons first.”
Although Ri repeated familiar North Korean complaints about Washington's resistance to the "hierarchical" approach to nuclear disarmament in which North Korea would be rewarded for every step it would take, his statement seemed special in that he did not deny unilateral nuclear disarmament cleanly as Pyongyang had done in the past.
Ri referred to the joint statement of Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump's joint statement at the first meeting between acting U.S. and North Korean leaders in Singapore on June 12, in which Kim vowed to work on the "nuclear disarmament of the Korean peninsula" and Trump issued a promise to ensure North Korea's security.
North Korea has sought relief to formally end the 1950-53 Korean War, but the United States has said Pyongyang must leave his nuclear weapons behind first.
Washington has also resisted charges of relaxing a strict trade embargo against North Korea.
"The United States insists that "nuclear disarmament takes place first" and exacerbates the pressure by using trade embargos to achieve it, and even protest against the declaration to end the war," Ri said.
“It’s only people who don’t know anything about us thinking that business bans can bring us to our knees.
But the problem is that continued trade embargos increase our distrust.”
Ri made no mention of plans for a second meeting between Kim and Trump that the U.S. leader spoke about at the UN earlier this week.
Instead, the minister spoke about three meetings between South Korean leader Kim and Moon Jae-In over the past five months, adding: "If the party to this nuclear disarmament case were South Korea instead of the United States, there wouldn't be this stand-off about nuclear disarmament on the Korean peninsula."
Despite this, the tone of Ri's speech was very different from last year's speech, when he told the UN General Assembly that it was unavoidable to target North Korea's ballistic missiles to the U.S. mainland after "the devilish president" Trump called Kim a "messeller" on a suicide mission.
Last year, Trump threatened to "destroy" North Korea, but this year he praised Kim at the United Nations for his courage in taking a step towards disarmament, but said there was still a lot of work to be done and that sanctions would have to continue until North Korea had destroyed all of its nuclear weapons.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn't have a timeframe in this regard, saying: "If it takes two years, three years, or five months - it doesn't matter."
China and Russia say the UN Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the steps it has taken.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the UN Security Council on Thursday that: "The Security Council's trade bans must continue to apply until complete nuclear disarmament has taken place and it has confirmed."
The Security Council was unanimous to exacerbate North Korea's trade embargo since 2006 in an attempt to block funding for Pyongyang's nuclear and missile programs.
Pompeo met Ri on the sidelines at the UN General Assembly and said afterward he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for another meeting.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip didn't go well.
He left Pyongyang in July and said the results had been achieved, but just hours later, North Korea condemned him for making "criminal demands."
North Korea pledged at a meeting with Moon this month to take down missile zones and a nuclear facility if the U.S. intervened "corresponding measures."
He said Kim had told him that the "corresponding actions" he sought were security assurances that Trump promised in Singapore and actions towards normal relationships with Washington.
Harvard students take classes at enough rest
A new course at the University of Harvard has provided all students with more sleep in an attempt to counterate the growing tendency of students to stay awake and study all night with the help of caffeine tablets.
A study found that students at the best universities in the world often know little or nothing when it comes to the basics of taking care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at the Harvard School of Medicine and a specialist at Brigham and the Women's Hospital, designed the course and believes it sees the first of its kind in the United States.
He got the idea of doing the course after giving a lecture on the impact that sleep deprivation has on learning ability.
"At the end of the lecture, a girl came up to me and asked, 'Why am I being told this first now, in my final year?'
She said no one had told her anything about the importance of sleep, which surprised me," he told the Telegraph.
The course, held for the first time this year, explains to students the basics of how good sleeping habits help learning ability and performance in sports and even promote general well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard's medical school and director of the school's health service, said the university had decided to promote the course after finding that students were extremely sleepless during the week.
The course is an hour long and involves various interactive projects.
In one part there is a picture of a dormitory room, where students click on a cup of coffee, curtains, sports shoes and books to learn about the effects of caffeine and light and how performance in sports can deteriorate with lack of sleep, along with the importance of a good routine for sleep.
In the second part, participants are told about how long-term lack of sleep can exacerbate the risk of heart attacks, stroke, depression and cancer.
A map across the school site with interactive icons then encourages participants to think about their daily habits.
“We don’t think it’s going to change student behavior at once.
But we think they have a right to know this, just as one has a right to know about the health effects of smoking cigarettes," Professor Czeisler added.
The culture that salutes those who stay awake and learn all night is still there, he said, adding that modern technology and ever-increasing pressure on students meant that sleep deprivation was a growing problem.
The guarantee of getting enough sleep of good quality should be a “secret weapon” of students in order to protect against stress, fatigue, and anxiety, he said – even to avoid gaining weight, because sleep deprivation increases the brain’s call for food so that the body is hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old student in chemistry and physiology from California, helped Professor Czeisler put the course together, but he sat one of the professor's courses last year during his first year at Harvard.
He said the course had opened his eyes and encouraged him to call for a course for all students at the school.
He hopes that the next step will be to ask all Master's students to complete a similar course before starting their studies at the university.
Professor Czeisler recommended that students contemplate setting an alarm clock, which reminds them to go to sleep, and to awaken them, and keep in mind the harmful effects from blue light emitted by electronic monitors and LED lights, but it can confuse the internal clock of the body and lead to trouble falling into sleep.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Mark from Menga shoots down Gerrard's men
Rangers still struggled on the outfield as Steven Gerrard's divisive team had to settle for a 1-0 loss at Livingston, following a goal from Dolly Menga.
Ibrox's team wanted to get their first win away after their victory at St Johnstone in February, but Gary Holt's team only gave Gerrard's team his second loss in his 18 games as manager. The team is now eight points behind Hearts, which is ranked at the top of Ladbrokes Premiership.
Menga scored seven minutes before half-time and Ranger's team seemed to lack any inspiration and was never likely to equalise.
Rangers now fall to sixth place but Livingston goes up to third and are solely behind Hibernian in goal difference.
Rangers could get into further trouble after lineman Calum Spence had to be treated because of a head injury after some object was thrown at him from the visitors' field half.
Gerrard made eight changes to the team that swept Ayr away and reached the semi-finals in the Betfred Cup.
Holt, on the other hand, stuck to the same Livi 11 set-up which reached a stage of Hearts last week and he would have been pleased with how his well-trained teammates rolled over the resistance.
Rangers were perhaps more involved with the ball, but Livingston created more opportunities with the ball it caught.
They should have scored just two minutes after the start of the game when the first pass from Menga arrived Scott Pitman in front of Allan McGregor's goal, but he shot past.
Keaghan Jacob's long free-kick then ended at Craig Halkett but Alan Lithgow, his defensive partner, shot past the far post.
Rangers regained control of the game but seemed to survive in hope rather than belief in their game in the final third of the game.
Alfredo Morales certainly felt he should have got a penalty when a quarter had passed of the game, when he and Steven Lawless collided but referee Steven Thomson withdrew the Colombian.
Rangers only managed to take two shots on goal in the first half but Liam Kelly, Ibrox's former goalkeeper, didn't have much to do with defending a header from Lassana Coulibaly and an easy shot from Ovie Ejaria.
Although an opportunity from Livi in the 34th minute would have come even though the other team had the upper hand, no one can deny that they deserved it, but only because of their perseverance.
Rangers failed again to do anything from Jacob's long free kick.
Scott Arfield didn't react when Declan Gallagher sent the ball onto Scott Robinson, who kept calm and chose to send on a Menga who then scored easily.
Gerrard took action at half-time and sent Ryan Kent in instead of Coulibaly and the switch had an impact almost immediately as the full-backer brought Morelos in, but Kelly reacted swiftly and ran forward to block the shot.
Livingston, however, continued to narrate the visitors to play exactly the type of game they enjoy playing and Lithgow and Halkett managed one long pass after another.
Holt's team could have exacerbated their lead in the final minutes, but McGregor did well in denying Jacobs a chance before Lithgow ran on goal from the corner.
Glenn Middleton, Ranger's deputy, wanted a second penalty when he and Jacobs collapsed, but Thomson dismissed it again.
Almanac: The man who invented the Geiger numerator
Here is a page from our “Sunday Morning”: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago today and FORWARD... this is the day the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method to detect and measure radioactivity and that invention eventually led to a device called Geiger meter.
Geiger has gained a solid place in the world of science but was also popular in pop culture, for example in the 1950 film “Bells of Coronado”, starringing Roy Rogers and Dale Evans in unlikely roles as scientists:
Man: "What the hell is that?"
Rogers: "It's a Geiger meter, used to find radioactive materials such as uranium.
When you put these headphones on, you can hear the effects from the atoms due to radioactivity in the rocks.”
Evans: "It's really cracking right now!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name lives on.
A new anti-cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” dangerous cells
A new anti-cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” dangerous cells and kill them
The vaccine teaches the immune system to recognise dangerous cells as part of the treatment
The treatment involves taking immune cells out of the patient and converting them to the laboratory
Then they can “see” proteins, which are common in many cancer cells, and are then injected back into the patient
An experimental vaccine has shown positive results in patients suffering from different types of cancer.
In one woman who received the vaccine, ovarian cancer disappeared for more than 18 months. The vaccine teaches immune cells to recognize dangerous cancer cells.
The method involves taking immune cells out of a patient and modifying them in a laboratory so they can “see” a protein called HER2 found in many types of cancer. Then the cells are injected back into the patient.
Professor Jay Berzofsky from the U.S. Institute of Cancer in Bethesday, Maryland, said: "Our findings suggest that the vaccine is extremely effective."
HER2 “has various types of cancer stopped growing,” including breast, ovaries, lungs, and colon and rectum, Professor Berzofsky said.
A similar method where immune cells are taken out of patients and “taught” to attack cancer cells has worked in the treatment of one type of leukemia.
Kanye West held a long monologue in support of Trump and wore a MAGA cap after appearing on the TV series SNL.
It didn't end well
Kanye West was buoyed in the studio on a Saturday Night Live episode after a lengthy speech in which he praised US President Donald Trump and said he would run for president in 2020.
After delivering the third song of the night, Ghost Town, in which he carried the Make America Great cap, he spoke for a long time against the Democrats and repeated his support for Trump.
"I've spoken so many times to white people who say, 'How can you like Trump, he's a racist?'
If I was worried about racism, I'd have moved out of the United States long ago," he said.
SNL began the show with a trailer in which Hollywood star Matt Damon joked about Brettur Kavanaugh's testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee over Christine Blasey Ford's allegations of sexual misconduct.
Although West's far-reaching lockdown wasn't sent out, her recording was posted on social media by comedian Chris Rock.
I don't know if Rock wanted to make fun of West by posting the recording.
West had also complained to the audience that he had received bad backstage receptions because of his hat selection.
“I was bullied backstage.
They said to me, “Don’t go up to the stage with that cap.”
They bullied me!
Then they say I'm in a deep hole," he said, according to the Washington Examiner.
West continued: “Do you want to see the deep hole?” and said he would put on his superhero cloak “because it means you can’t give me what to do. Do you want the world to make progress?
“Try love.”
His remarks were met with a buzz from the audience at least twice, and SNL's actors seemed embarrassed, according to Variety, and one of the attendees said to the newspaper: "There was stone silence in the studio."
West had been invited to come on the show instead of singer Ariana Grande, but her boyfriend, rapper Mac Miller, had died a few days earlier.
West surprised many with his stage presence as he sang the song I Love it, dressed like a bottle of Perrier.
West received support from Candace Turner, chairman of TPUSA's Conservative Party, who tweeted: "To one of the most courageous souls: THANK YOU FOR SHOWING THE GROUND OF RESISTS."
But talk show host Karen Hunter tweeted that West was simply being "him and it's absolutely wonderful."
“But I chose not to reward someone (by buying his music or clothing or supporting his ‘art’) that I feel supports and spreads an ideology that is detrimental to my society.
- He's free.
That's what we are, too," she added.
Before the show, the rapper announced on Twitter that he had changed his name and said it was "the creature previously known as Kanye West."
He's not the first artist to change his name and follow in Diddy's footsteps, which is also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
Another rapper, Snoop Dogg, has also called himself Snoop Lion and must not be overlooked by the musical myths Prince, who turned his name into a symbol and later called himself the artist who was formerly known as Prince.
Prosecution for attempted murder after knife attack at a restaurant in Belfast
A 45-year-old man has been charged with attempted murder after a man was stabbed at a restaurant in eastern Belfast on Tuesday.
Ballyhackamore had the incident, according to police.
The defendant is expected to appear in a courtroom in Belfast on Monday.
Prosecutors will review the charges.
Kit Harington, the star of the Crown Games, criticizes toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is known for his role as Jon Snow in the violent adventure series from HBO.
However, the 31-year-old actor has criticized the stereotype about the masculine hero, saying that such roles on screen mean that young boys often feel that they need to be tough in order to gain respect.
In an interview with the Sunday Times Culture, Kit said he believes "something has gone wrong" and wondered how to deal with the problem of toxic masculinity during the #MeToo era.
Kit, who recently married his co-star from the Crown Games, 31-year-old Rose Leslie, acknowledged that he had "strong views" on how to deal with this issue.
“Personally, I think at the moment – what’s gone wrong with masculinity?”
“What have we blamed men growing up, about the problems we see now?”
Kit believes that television is partly responsible for an increase in toxic masculinity, due to an extremely masculine character on television.
He continued: "What is congenital and what is taught?
What is taught on TV and on the streets that makes young boys feel like they have to be a certain type of man?
I think that's one of the most important questions of our time - how do we change this?
“It’s obvious that something has gone wrong for young men.”
He also acknowledged in the interview that he would not star in any other animated series after next summer's season, saying he'd had "enough of battlefields and horses."
As of November, Kit will star in the re-creation of True West, Sam Shepard's film, which tells the story of a film producer and his robber.
The actor recently said the best thing about having starred in the Crown Games was to have met his wife, Rose.
"I met my wife in this show, so they pretty much gave me my future family and my life after that," he said.
Rose played Ygritte's lover Jon Snow as Kit in these Emmy Awards.
The couple married in June 2018, on the land of Lesli's family in Scotland.
HIV: China announces 14% increase in new infections
China has reported a 14% increase in the number of people infected with the HIV virus or having AIDS.
Health officials say more than 820,000 people are infected in the country.
About 40,000 new infections were reported only in the second quarter of 2018.
The vast majority of new infections came with sex, which is a change from what it was before.
In the past, the HIV virus spread rapidly in some parts of parts of China due to donors of infected blood.
However, the number of people infected with the HIV virus in this way has fallen to almost zero, according to Chinese health officials at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, the number of people infected with HIV or AIDS has increased by 100,000 people from year to year.
An HIV infection caused by sex is a serious problem within the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality has not been illegal in China since 1997, but widespread discrimination against LGBT people still exists.
Due to conservative values in the country, studies have estimated that 70-90% of men who have sex with other men eventually marry a woman.
Many infections arise due to insufficient contraception in these marriages.
Since 2003, China's government has pledged access for everyone to HIV medicines as part of a plan to address the problem.
Maxine Waters denies that an employee leaked evidence from Republican senators, criticizes "dangerous lies" and "conspiracy theories"
Maxine Waters, a congresswoman, against Saturday denied allegations that one of her employees had disclosed the personal details of three Senate Republican Republicans on their Wikipedia pages.
The Los Angeles Democrat said these claims came from "super-right" extremists and websites.
"Lies, lies and other pathetic lies," Waters said in a statement on Twitter.
According to, the information provided contained the addresses and phone numbers of U.S. senators Lindsey Graham from South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch.
The information appeared online on Thursday from an unchanned party on the Capitol Hill during interrogations by the Senate on Brett Kavanaugh, a nomined Supreme Court justice, over allegations of sexual harassment.
The leak came sometime after the three senators had questioned Kavanaugh.
Conservative sites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState said the IP addresses that identified the source of the information were linked to Waters' office and published information about one of its employees, according to Capitol Hill.
"This unfounded accusation is an absolutely false and pure lie," Waters continued.
“My employee – whose personal information and security is now at risk from these false accusations – was in no way responsible for this information leakage.
“This unfounded accusation is completely false and pure lies.”
Water's statement was quickly criticized online, including by Ara Fleischer, a former White House correspondent.
"This denial is full of anger," Fleischer wrote.
"It suggests she doesn't have the mood to sit in parliament.
When someone is blamed for no reason, they must not react with anger.
You can't show opposition.
You can't ask about the motives of the accused.
You have to show calmness and calmness.”
Fleischer appeared to compare Water's reaction to the Democrat's critique of Judge Kavanaugh, which critics said appeared to be too angry at Thursday's trial.
Omar Navarro, the Republican candidate against Waters in the midterm elections, also voiced his opinion on Twitter.
“It’s a big deal if it’s true,” he tweeted.
In his statement, Waters said her office had alerted "appropriate authorities and law enforcement officers of these false claims.
“We will make sure that the guilty will be found,” she continued, “and that they will be prosecuted for all the actions that are destructive and dangerous for all my employees.”
Johnny English Strikes again - Rowan Atkinson's spicy comedy turns out to be powerless
It's become a habit to look for Brexit references in all the new films where the UK is involved, and it seems relevant to this resuscitation of the Johnny English comedy series - which started in 2003 with the movie Johnny English and just woke up back to life in 2011 with the movie Johnny English Reborn.
Will this satire about how clearly pathetic we are being a new export opportunity for the country?
Whether or not, Johnny English has been granted renewed permission for the second time to mess up everything - his name shows above all that he is a creation designed for an exhibition in other than English-speaking countries.
Of course, he's a wise secret agent who pretends to possess breathtaking charm but is a bit of a mix of Clouseau, a dash of Mr. Bean and a little splash of the man who played one note for the song Chariots of Fire at the opening ceremony of the 2012 London Olympics in 2012.
It's also originally based on the traveler and mysterious cosmopolitan that Atkinsons once starred in the forgotten Barclaycard television commercials, in which he divorced everything in chaos.
There's one or two good scenes in this latest Johnny English film.
I enjoyed seeing Johnny English walk to a helicopter wearing a medieval armor and the helicopter blades fell into his helmet.
Atkinson clearly demonstrates his comedy skills, but the humor seems rather powerless and bizarrely superficial, especially already “serious” films such as James Bond and Mission Impossible films also offer without hesitation a joke.
The comedy seems intended for children rather than adults, and I found the adventures that Johnny English encountered not as imaginative and focused as Atkinson's silent comedians in Mr. Beans.
The perpetual premise of the film now is that the UK is in a lot of trouble.
A hacker has broken into Britain's secret spy net and detected the identity of all British spies, to a great deal for the spy who is on duty - but that's where Kevin Eldon unfortunately performs in too small a role.
It's the finale for the prime minister who's an arrogant and controversial character and already in great dilements because of immense political unpopularity: Emma Thompson does her best with her role as a character that's supposed to be reminiscent of Theresa May, but the script isn't up to many fish for her.
Her advisers tell her that all active spies are in danger and that she has to find someone who had settled down.
And that's where Johnny English comes into play, now the headmaster of some fancy school, but also provides secret instruction in how to be a spy: here are a few funny jokes that English offers a spy school reminiscent of the film School of Rock.
English is summoned back to Whitehall during an emergency meeting and meets Bough, his former long-suffering partner, played by Ben Miller.
Bough is now a married man, his wife is a submarine captain, but in that energetic role Vicki Pepperdine doesn't quite let his light shine.
The spy duo that messes everything up in Her Majesty's service is now back in gear and meets high-profile Ophelia Bulletova, played by Olga Kurylenko.
Simultaneously, the prime minister is captivated by the magic of the intriguing billionaire in the technology sector, who claims to be able to solve Britain's computer problems: it's the evil Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough begin their devastating missions: they ignite a fancy French restaurant disguised as waiters, they cause havoc as they smuggle themselves into Volta's luxury yacht, and English turns everything upside down when he tries to use virtual reality goggles to familiarize himself with the infrastructure in Volta's house.
There's no saving in that final scene, but even though the picture is playful and energetic, there's quite a bit of children's material in it as a whole.
Moderate material.
And just as with the other Johnny English films, I couldn't help but think: can the British film industry let Rowan Atkinson get a role that really suits his skills?
Labour denies it is creating a plan for a four-day working week for the British, with the same salary as for five days a week.
Labour, led by Jeremy Corbyn, is now contemplating a radical plan that would allow Britain to work for four days a week, but get paid for five.
Accordingly, the party wants to let corporate bosses take advantage of the savings generated from the revolution in artificial intelligence, and let employees benefit by getting one extra holiday.
Then employees could enjoy a three-day-long weekend, without a reduction in wages.
Sources say the idea would "fit" the party's economic plan and its strategy to improve the interests of workers.
The trade union Congress has supported the shortening of the working week for four days, as a way for workers to take advantage of changes in the economy.
A reporter from Labour's upper divisions told the Sunday Times: "It is estimated that a policy review will be announced before the end of the year.
“The change will not happen overnight, but a four-day working week is a goal that matches the party’s approach to changing the economy for the benefit of workers and to the party’s overall industrial policy.”
Labour won't be the party's first party to support this idea, but the Green Party promised a four-day working week in its election campaign in 2017.
However, the goal is not supported by the Labour Party as a whole.
Labour spokeswoman said: "Four-day working weeks are not on the party's manifesto, and the party is not considering implementing it."
John McDonnell, the Commerce Secretary of the Shadow Ministry, used Labour's conference last week to describe his notions of a social revolution within the economy.
Mr. McDonnell said he was determined to regain power from "unknown CEOs" and "commercial collectors".
The shadow minister's plans also mean that existing shareholders in water supply companies may not get their entire stake back as Labour's government could impose "deductions" due to alleged wrongdoing.
He has also confirmed plans to put workers on corporate management committees and create a joint ownership fund to let 10 percent of private companies' assets to their staff, which could get a 500-pound dividend from them annually in their pockets.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy say "60 minutes" whether the FBI's investigation into Kavanaugh could make them change their minds
The FBI's investigation into allegations against Judge Brett Kavanaugh has caused at least a week-long delay in a final vote on his nomination to the Supreme Court, and raises the question of whether its findings could get Senate Republicans to withdraw their support.
In an interview that will be shown on Sunday, Scott Pelley, a reporter on the show "60 Minutes" Republicans and senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham asked if the FBI could reveal anything that would make them change their minds.
Kennedy appeared to be more open than his colleague from South Carolina.
"I mean, of course," Kennedy said.
"I said when the trial began, I said I spoke to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him after the allegations were filed and asked, "Did you do this?"
He was determined, steadfast, unequivocal.”
Graham's vote, however, seems unchangeable.
"I've made up my mind about Brett Kavanaugh and it would take an incredible accusation to change that," he said.
"Dr. Ford, I don't know what happened, but this is what I know: Brett firmly denied it," Graham added, addressing Christine Blasey Ford.
"And everyone she mentions by name couldn't confirm it.
It's 36-year-old.
“I don’t think anything new will change.”
What is the International Citizens Festival and has it done anything to reduce poverty?
The Global Citizen Festival will be held on Saturday in New York. It is an annual music event in which a series of superstars will emerge and with a similarly impressive strategy; to end poverty around the world.
This is the seventh year that the International Civic Festival is held and tens of thousands of people will flock to Central Park, not only to listen music scenes from, among other things, Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the festival's real goal of ending extreme poverty by 2030.
The International Citizens' Festival, which was first held in 2012, is part of the Global Poverty Project (Global Poverty Project), an international advocacy organization that wants to end poverty by increasing the number of people fighting it.
In order to receive free tickets to the event (except for those who want to pay for VIP tickets), concertgoers had to complete various tasks, or “actions”, such as doing volunteer work, sending an e-mail to any of the world’s leaders, making a phone call or using other valid ways to help raise awareness of their goal of ending poverty.
But how much success has the festival achieved, now that 12 years exist a strategy to achieve the goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with free concerts in a real way to convince people to call for action, or is this just but one so-called “click action” – where people feel they do something that matters by signing an online petition or sending a message on Twitter?
According to 2011, it has recorded more than 19 million “actions” from its supporters, calling for a variety of different goals.
It says these initiatives have helped get world leaders to declare commitments and policies equivalent to over US$35 billion that will affect the lives of over 2.25 billion people by 2030.
In the early part of 2018, the group listed 390 commitments and announcements regarding its actions, of which at least 10 billion dollars have been invested or accumulated.
The group estimates that the funds accumulated so far have directly affected nearly 649 million people around the world.
One of the main commitment comes from The Power of Nutrition, an association of investors and chief executive in the UK that works to "help children to enjoy themselves to the fullest," and pledged to supply 35 million dollars to Rwanda to help end malnutrition in the country, after receiving 4700 messages on Twitter from Global Citizens.
"With the support of the UK government, sponsors, governments around the world and international citizens like yourselves, we can let the societal injustice that malnutrition is of the past," Tracey Ullmann, a representative of The Power of Nutrition, told the audience at a concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that after 5000 calls were sent that the UK should improve nutrition for mothers and children, the government announced the funding of a project, the Power of Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children and provide them with better nutrition.
On the list of frequently asked questions on the group's website, one of the questions is as follows: “Why do you think you can put an end to extreme poverty?”
Global citizen replied: "It will be a long and difficult task - sometimes we will fail all the time.
But, we will achieve our goal, just like movements that fought for the rights of citizens and the abolition of racial segregation ahead of us, because we are stronger together.
Among those performing at this year's event are Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, and Janelle Monáe. The event will be held in New York and the presenters are Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
The United States could use the Navy as a "obstruction" to impede Russia's energy exports," the interior minister said
“If necessary,” Washington could use the Navy to prevent Russia’s energy from gaining on the market, including in the Middle East, U.S. Interior Minister Ryan Zinke said, according to a Washington Examiner report.
Zinke argued that Russia's assistance to Syria, especially since the country works at the invitation of the legitimate government there, would be an excuse to explore new markets.
“I think the reason for their presence in the Middle East is that they want to do business with energy such as in Eastern Europe and in southern Europe,” he said.
And, according to the official announcement, there are ways and ways to combat it.
"The United States can use the Navy to ensure that maritime routes are open and, if necessary, to ensure that their energy does not reach the market," he said.
Zinke addressed a gathering hosted by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a charity that calls itself the “voice of energy consumer” in the United States.
He went on and compared Washington's approach to dealing with Russia and Iran and said it was more or less the same.
"The economic option for Iran and Russia is more or less the exploitation of energy sources and replacing them with others," he said, claiming that Russia has just one creek with an economy that would depend on fossil fuels.
The statements come in the wake of Trump's administration wanting to increase its exports of natural gas to Europe and replace Russia, which is a much cheaper option for European consumers.
To do this, civil servants in the Trump administration, including President Donald Trump himself, have tried to get Germany to withdraw from participation in the "inappropriate" Nord Stream 2 mission to impose a new energy pipeline, which Trump says would make Berlin a "detain" Moscow.
Moscow has repeatedly argued that the 11 billion dollar Nord Stream 2 energy pipeline, which will double the transmission capacity of the current pipeline to 110 billion cubic meters, is purely an economic project.
The Russians say Washington's strong opposition against the project is simply driven by economic reasons and that it is an example of unfair competition.
“I believe we share the view that energy must not be a tool to apply pressure and that consumers should be able to opt for the suppliers,” Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said after a meeting with U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry in Moscow in September.
America's position has been criticised by Germany, which has confirmed its commitment to the project.
Germany's leading industry association, the Federation of German Industries (BDI), has urged the United States not to interfere in the EU's energy policy and a bilateral agreement between Berlin and Moscow.
"I'm very unhappy that outside countries interfered in our energy supply," Dieter Kempf, chairman of the Federation of German Industries (BDI), said after a meeting recently with German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warren will 'considere seriously' to run for the presidency in 2020," she said
Massachusetts Senator Elizabeth Warren said on Saturday that she would "consider seriously" running for president after the midterm elections.
Warren confirmed she would consider volunteering at a civil meeting in Holyoke, Massachusetts.
"It's time for women to go to Washington and track our government, and then it'll also take a woman as a leader," she said.
"After November 6, I will seriously consider running for president."
Warren talked about President Donald Trump at the meeting and said he was "taking the country into the wrong direction.
"I'm very concerned about what Donald Trump is doing to our democracy," she said.
Warren has been unumbered by his critique of Trump and Brett Kavanahugh's nomined Supreme Court.
On Friday, Warren tweeted that "of course, we must let the FBI investigate the matter before the vote."
However, an opinion poll published on Thursday shows that a majority of voters in Warren's state don't feel she should be volunteering in 2020.
Fifty-eight percent of more "probable" voters in Massachusetts said the senator shouldn't volunteer, according to a poll published by the Suffolk University Political Research Center/Boston Globe.
Thirty-two percent said they supported her candidacy.
The poll showed more support for Deval Patrick's candidacy as 38 percent said they supported his possible candidacy and 48 percent said they opposed it.
Among other popular Democrats interviewed for a possible presidential campaign in 2020 included Joe Biden, a former vice president, and Bernie Sanders, a senator from Vermont.
Biden said he would make an official decision in January, the Associated Press reported.
