Track Palin, 26, was in Iraq for one year after joining forces in September.
He was arrested and charged with domestic violence on Monday night
"My son is going through a lot of hardship after coming back, I feel sorry for other families affected by post-traumatic stress disorder and the psychological wounds that our soldiers return home with," she told the audience at an electoral meeting for Donald Trump in Tulsa, Oklahoma.
Palin called his "elephant elephant in the room" and said that her son and other war soldiers "come back a little differently, they're tough and wonder if there's any respect for what their members in the army and the air forces and other armies have done for the country.""
He was arrested on Monday in Wasilla, Alaska and charged with domestic violence against a woman, for attempting to obstruct the reporting of domestic violence and for carrying weapons under the influence of alcohol, according to Dan Bennett, a spokesman for the police department in Wasilla.
18 states and Washington D.C. support challenge against new asylum seeker policy
Eighteen states and the City of Washington support a legal challenge against new U.S. policies that denies victims fleeing gang or domestic violence for asylum.
Representatives of states 18 and the city submitted a forensic report on Friday in Washington to support an asylum seeker who opposes the policy, according to NBC News reports.
The defendant's full name in the Grace v. Sessions lawsuit put forward by the American Civil Liberties Union has not been disclosed.
She said her spouse "and his abusive sons who were migrant members," sexually assaulted her, while U.S. government officials rejected her application for asylum on July 20.
She was detained in Texas.
The prosecutors, who support Grace, described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala as countries where problems related to gangs and domestic violence are constantly increasing. The number of asylum seekers to the United States comes from these countries.
The new asylum seeker policy revoked a ruling issued by the Immigration Appeals Committee in 2014 to allow unregistered immigrants who fled domestic violence to apply for asylum.
Washington Attorney General Karl Racine said in a statement on Friday that the new policy "ignored more decades of federal and international law."
"The federal law requires a decision on all asylum applications based on the facts and circumstances of the application, and this policy violates this rule," the legal report said.
Lawyers further stated in the report that policies that denied immigrants entry were detrimental to America's economy, and argued that immigrants were more likely to start a business and "provided an important workforce."
Attorney General Jeff Sessions ordered immigration judges to stop granting asylum to victims who fled domestic violence and gang violence in June.
"Those who leave their home country because of persecution based on race, religion, nationality or membership of a particular social group or political opinion can apply for asylum," Sessions said in a June 11 statement.
Asylum services were never intended to solve all problems, not even all serious problems, that people deal with every day around the world.
Desperate rescue efforts in a race to find survivors in Palu, the number of dead has doubled
Circumstances are becoming increasingly difficult for survivors.
"There's a lot of excitement in the air," Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, said as she comforted her sick son at a relief center in the city of Palu.
“Sickers bring bodies here all the time.
“There’s very little clean water.”
Residents were seen returning to their ruined homes and searching their watershed possessions to try to save what they could find.
Hundred people were injured and hospitals are overcrowded and damaged after an earthquake measuring 7.5 on the Richter scale.
Some of the wounded, including Dwi Haris, who fractured their back and shoulders, lay outside Palu's military hospital, which favored patients outdoors due to continued powerful aftershocks.
Tears filled his eyes as he told how he felt the earthquake shaking his hotel room on the fifth floor, where he was with his wife and daughter.
"There was no time to save ourselves.
"I'm pushing up to the ruins," Haris told the Associated Press and said his family had been in town to go to a wedding.
“I heard my wife call for help, but then silence.”
I don't know what happened to her and my daughter.
"I hope they're safe."
U.S. ambassador accuses China of "persecution" with 'propaganda advertising'
A week after an official newspaper in China published a four-page advertisement in a U.S. newspaper to cover mutual advantages in trade between the two countries, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of exploiting U.S. media to spread propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump spoke last Wednesday about the Chinese newspaper's advertisement in the newspaper Des Moines Register - which is the best-selling newspaper in Iowa - after accusing China of trying to intervene in the U.S. parliamentary elections on November 6. China denies this allegation.
Trump's accusation that Beijing wanted to intervene in the U.S. congressional election marked what official U.S. delegates told Reuters news agency that there was a new stage in an expansive campaign from Washington to pressure China.
While it's normal for foreign governments to publish advertisements to promote trade, Beijing and Washington are facing an ever-harding trade war, with new tariffs on imports of both countries.
Chinese and U.S. analysts said China's retaliatory tariffs early in the trade war were designed to harm exporters in states such as Iowa, which backed Trump's Republican Party.
Terry Branstad's U.S. ambassador to China and former governor of Iowa for the long term, but that state exports a lot of agricultural products to China, said Beijing had harmed U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
Branstad said in an incoming commentary in the Sunday edition of the Des Moines Register that "China is now increasing its persecution by publishing propaganda advertisements in our own free press."
"By spreading its propaganda, China's government is taking advantage of America's beloved freedom of speech tradition and our free press by publishing a sponsored advertisement in the Des Moines Register," Branstad said in an article.
“On the other hand, there are very few protest voices in newspaper booths on the streets of Beijing, and there can be no true reflection of the different views that the Chinese might have on China’s concerned economic policies, as the media is stuck under the heels of the Chinese Communist Party,” he wrote.
He added that “one of China’s biggest newspapers escaped the offer to publish” his article, but he didn’t specify which newspaper it was.
Analysts warn that Republicans are making female voters obscure them before the midterm elections with the Kavanaugh affair.
Analysts have warned of negative reactions, especially from women, in the next midterm elections, because many Republicans have supported and defended Brett Kavanaugh, a nomined Supreme Court justice, who has faced accusations of sexual harassment.
There have been very strong emotions around this issue and most Republicans have already shown publicly that they wanted to start voting.
"These things can't be reversed," Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at the Maxwell School at Syracuse, told The Hill for an article released on Saturday.
Reeher said he doubted that Jeff Flake, the Republican senator in Arizona, in the last minute of an FBI investigation was enough to calm angry voters.
“Women won’t forget what happened yesterday, they won’t forget about it tomorrow and not in November,” said Karine Jean-Pierre, senior adviser and spokeswoman for the Liberal group MoveOn.
On Friday morning, protesters "November is approaching!" as they protested in the corridors of the Senate when the Republicans who ruled the ruling committee decided to proceed with Kavanugh's nomination, despite the testimony of Dr. Christine Blasey Ford, according to Mic.
"The interest and enthusiasm of the electorate will arise," Stu Rothenberg, an independent political analyst, told the news website.
"People will say the interest has already been huge and that's true.
But he could be greater, especially among female voters in the suburbs and younger voters, 18-29 years old, who doesn’t like the president, but rarely votes.”
Analysts predicted a setback if Republicans pushed to have Kavanaugh ratified, even for Ford's testimonies in which she described her allegations of sexual harassment from him.
"It's become a big mess for the Republican Party," said Michael Still, a former member of the Republican National Committee, early last week, according to NBC News.
"It's not just about the committee's vote or the final vote or whether Kavanaugh will be left sitting on the bench, it's also about how Republicans have handled it and how they've treated it," Guy Cecil, chairman of Priorities USA, a group that helps elect Democrats, told the news channel.
However, Americans seem to disagree with who to believe after testimonials from both Ford and Kavanaugh, and a small majority supports the latter.
A new poll from YouGov shows that 41 percent of respondents definitely or probably believed the testimonials from Ford, while 35 percent said they believed safely or probably Kavanaugh's testimonials.
In addition, 38 percent of respondents said they believed Kavanaugh probably or definitely lied in their testimony, while only 30 percent said the same thing about Ford.
After Flake's appeal, the FBI is now investigating the allegations put forward by Ford and from at least one other woman, Deboruh Ramirez, according to a Guardian report.
Ford testified before the Senate Judiciary Committee last week, saying Kavanaugh had assaulted her when she was 17 and that he had been under the influence of alcohol.
Ramirez says Kavanaugh carried her genitals in front of her when they were both at a party at Yale University in the 1980s.
The man who invented the Internet has plans to start a new internet to combat Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the man who invented the internet, plans to start a start-up to combat Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt's last project, Inrupt, is a company based on Berners-Lee's open platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is saved and which people are allowed to access any information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee jokingly said the intention behind Inrupt was "world domination."
"We have to do this right now," he said of the start-up.
"It's a historic moment."
The app uses Stolid’s technology to enable people to create their own “personal online data store” or POD.
There you can have a list of contacts, to-do lists, calendars, a music library and other personal and work-related devices.
Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are all located in a single browser and all simultaneously.
The unique thing about the personal data store is that it is totally up to the user who can access any information.
The company calls it “personal empowerment by using data.”
According to John Bruce, the company's CEO, the idea to Inrupt is that the company provides inputs, processes, and relevant skills to help making Solid accessible to everyone.
Right now, the company consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform that was acquired by IBM, several programmers who were hired as contractors to work on the project, and a group of volunteer developers.
As of this week, technology developers around the world could create their own independent programs with the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team shouldn't have discussions with "Facebook and Google about whether to trigger an absolute change that would put their current business model on the head overnight."
“We don’t ask for permission.”
Berners-Lee wrote in an article published Saturday on Medium that Inrupt had “the goal of creating energy in business and ecosystems that helps protect the honesty and quality of the new site that is based on Solid.”
Berners-Lee revolutionized the network in 1994 when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In the last few months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the debate over the net's neutrality.
Berners-Lee remains the founder and CEO of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation of the Open Data Institute, even while establishing Inrupt.
"I'm full of optimism for the next season of the network," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Won: Pastor and holder of the Victorian Cross of the First World Wars honored
The only English priest to be honored with the Victorian Cross during the First World Warris as a soldier has been saluted in his hometown 100 years later.
Reverend Bernard, who served as U.S. Army, received the Cross on September 29, 1918, during the attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
However, the sniper killed him four days later and he never got to know that he had been awarded the British army's highest medal.
His two grandsons unveiled a monument about him during a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire, on Saturday.
One of his grandsons, Michael Vann, said it was "extremely symbolic" that the memorial was unveiled exactly 100 years after his grandfather's honorary achievement.
According to the London Gazette newspaper, Colonel Vann led his army on September 29, 1911 over the Canal de Saint-Quentin canal "through extremely thick fog and under a hard-fire shooting from cannons and machine guns."
Later he ran to the firing line and with "great courage" he led the line forward before attacking the cannon of one of his troops and overpowering three soldiers.
The undercoats were killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, just a month before the end of the war.
The 72-year-old said his grandfather's exploits were "something I could never do, but something that humbles you."
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, also laid flower wreaths after the parade, which was led by the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said he felt "great honor to take part in the parade", adding that "the admirers of a true hero are shown with the support that so many people show."
MMA fans woke up all night to watch Bellator 206, got in return to see Gurru a piggy
Just imagine, having awakened throughout the night to see an overcrowded Bellator 206, just to be denied to see the main event.
The program included 13 fights from San Jose, six of which on the merchancy and they were broadcast live throughout the night in the UK on the Channel 5 channel.
At 6 o'clock in the morning, just when Gegard Mousasi and Rory McDonald were preparing to fight, British spectators received an unexpected surprise when the broadcast turned into a Gurra pig.
Some were unhappy after watching all night to watch the fight.
One fan on Twitter described the switch to children's content as "some kind of sick joke."
"According to official rules, the material was not suitable for viewers at six o'clock in the morning so they had to switch to children's content," said Dave Schwartz, deputy director of marketing and communications for Bellator, when asked about the broadcast.
“Gurra pig, yes.”
Bellator's CEO Scott Coker said they would review their timetables and take viewers in the UK into account in the future.
"I think when I think of the replay that we could probably resolve this," Coker said.
"But it's six o'clock in the morning on a Sunday and we can't resolve this until Sunday, which is their Monday.
But we're working on it.
Believe me, by the time there was a change of transmission, numerous text messages were sent back and forth, and they weren't all friendly.
We were trying to fix this, we thought this was a technical problem.
But it wasn't, it was an official problem.
I can promise you, this won't happen next time.
We limit the evening to five battles instead of six, as usual, but we tried too hard and got ahead of ourselves.
“It’s an unfortunate situation.”
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt 'incompetence' because of sexual orientation
Olympic swimmer Tom Daley says he felt powerless towards other people growing up because of his sexual orientation, but that it encouraged him to succeed.
The 24-year-old Daley said he didn't realize "he's different from anyone else" until he went to secondary school."
On the radio show Desert Island Discs on the Radio 4 channel, with Lauren Laverne, Daley told Daley that he spoke about gay rights to give others “hope.”
He also said that parenthood has diminished his interest in winning the Olympics.
Kirsty Young, the regular moderator, has taken a few months off due to illness.
Daley turned out on Laverne’s first episode and said he felt he was a “less man” than everyone else when he was a child because “it wasn’t agreed in the community to be attracted to both boys and girls.”
He said: “Up until today, these feelings of feeling that I am inferior and otherwise have given me the power and strength to succeed.”
He wanted to prove that he was "something," he said, so he wouldn't disappoint anyone when his sexuality was finally discovered.
The two-time Olympic bronze holder has made a huge impact as a supporter of the LGBT and used his participation in Australia's Commonwealth Games to call on more nations to legalize homosexuality.
He said he said he would be lucky to be able to live in public without punishment and wanted to give others “hope.”
The world's triple champion said it had "surprised him" to fall in love with a man - American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he first met in 2013.
Daley married the Oscar winner, who is 20 years older than him, last year but said the age difference had never been a problem.
“When one goes through so much at a young age” – but he took part in his first Olympic Games at the age of 14 and his father died of cancer three years later – he said it was difficult to find peers who had experienced similar success and hardship.
The couple adopted a child in June, a son named Robert Ray Black-Daley and Daley said his "all views of life" had cuvenished.
"If you'd asked me last year, it's all about winning a gold medal," he said.
“That’s more important than a gold medal at the Olympics.
Robbie is my Olympic gold medal.”
His son is christened at the head of Robert, his father who died in 2011 at the age of forty, after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said his father didn't accept that he was going to die and one of the last things he'd asked about was whether they've got their tickets for London in 2012 - because he wanted to sit in the front row.
"I couldn't tell him he wouldn't live long enough to sit in the front row," he said.
"I held his hand when he stopped breathing, and it wasn't until his breath stopped and he was dead that I finally admitted he wasn't invincible," he said.
The following year, Daley competed at the 2012 Olympics and won a bronze medal.
"I just knewed that I'd been dreaming about it all my life, dipping me in front of spectators at home during the Olympics. It doesn't feel like a better feeling," he said.
There was also an inspiration for the selection of the first song - Heather Small's Proud - which had inspired him for the Olympics and still gave him goose bumps.
Desert Island Discs is on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11.15 p.m.
Mickelson in bad shape and left to sit on the bench in the Ryder Cup on Saturday
Phil Mickelson's U.S. will break a record on Sunday as he plays his 47th game in the Ryder Cup, but he has to find back his old rhythms so that the record isn't surrounded by bad memories.
Mickelson, who plays in the competition for the 12th time a record, was dropped on the bench following the decision of captain Jim Furyk for the competitions in four-ball and four-time matches on Saturday.
Instead of being the center of action, as it has been so many times before for the United States of America, the five-time champion divided the day between being a cheerleader and practicing the rhythms on the pitch in the hope of correcting what he likes.
Mickelson, 48, was never the one who shot directly with the drivers, and isn't the best player for the Le Golf National golf course, as long bunkers often punish bad shots.
And not only that the golf course is a big challenge in itself, but Mickelson has to compete against Frances Molinari, the winner of the British Open, in the ninth game on Sunday. Molinari is with newcomer Tommy Fleetwood in the team and together they have won all four of their games this week.
If the U.S
Furyk expressed confidence in his man, but he could hardly say much else.
"He perfectly understood the role he had today, patted me on the back and took care of me and said he'll be ready tomorrow," Furyk said.
"He's got a lot of confidence.
He's in the Hall of Fame and he's been such an improvement for the teams before and this week.
I don't think he'd play two games.
I envisioned more, but that's how it ended and we thought we had to go this way.
“He wants to be out there, just like everyone else.”
Mickelson will beat Nick Faldo's record in a number of played matches in the Ryder Cup on Sunday.
This could mark the end of a career in the Ryder trophy which has never achieved quite as high as other achievements on his record.
Mickelson has 18 victories, 20 losses and 7 draws, while Furyk said his presence had a definitely vague effect on the team.
"He's funny, he's cynical, witty, he likes to make fun of people and it's great to have him in the team room," he explained.
"I think the younger players enjoyed teasing him too this week, and it was fun to see.
“He’s given so much more than just golf.”
Thomas Bjorn, Europe's captain, knows that a huge advantage can disappear quickly
European captain Thomas Bjorn knows from experience that a good advantage for the last day of the individual competition in the Ryder Cup can easily turn into an unpleasant experience.
The Danes first played in a game in Valderrama in 1997, where a side led by Seve Ballesteros had a five-point advantage over the Americans, but managed to squeeze out a win at the end with the slightest possible scoring, with a 141⁄2-131⁄2 victory.
“You keep reminding ourselves that we had a huge advantage in Valderrama, we had a huge advantage in Brookline where we lost and in Valderrama, where we won, but only just so,” said Bjorn, who can be seen here in the picture, after watching the 2018 cohort win 5-3 both on Friday and Saturday and gain an advantage 10-6 on the Le Golf National course.
The story will show me and everyone on the team that it's not over.
We're gonna make every effort tomorrow.
Let's do everything right.
This isn't over until all the points are on the table.
We have our goals and it's trying to win this trophy, we're focused on that
“I’ve always said this, I’m focused on the 12 players on our team, but we’re very aware of our opponents on the other team – the best golfers in the world.”
Bjorn was thrilled with how his players had performed on a difficult pitch and added: "I'll never get ahead of myself.
Tomorrow's going to be completely different.
Tomorrow it's the performance of the individuals that matters, and that's another thing.
It's great to play with a partner when everything goes well, but when you play alone, every man's talent is tested as a golfer.
That's the message that has to be sent to the players, to get the best out of themselves tomorrow.
“He’s going to have to play his best game as well.”
Unlike Bjorn, his opponent Jim Furyk will seek that his players perform better in individual games rather than with partners, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who won three points out of four.
Furyk himself has experienced both sides of this major transition on the last day, but he was victorious with his team at Brookline but ended up losing when Europe carried out the "miracle in Medinah."
"I remember every fucking word," he replied when asked how Ben Crenshaw in 1999 had motivated his players for the last day.
"We have to play 12 important games tomorrow, but you want to start very quickly, as it was at Brookline and Medinah.
As that landslide manages to build up, it causes a lot of pressure on the games that are in the middle.
“We’re sorting our players and let them play like we think they’re going best tomorrow.”
Thomas has been tasked with trying to lead the defence and plays against Rory McIlroy in the first game, with Paull Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter as the other European players in the first half of the games.
"I chose these players in that order because I think they'll tire all the way through," Bjorn said.
Germany's new warship postponed once again
The German Navy's latest warship should have been built in 2014 to replace old warships from the Cold War period, but it won't be ready until next year at the earliest due to malfunctioning systems and ever-increasing costs, according to local news reports.
The settlement of "Rheinland-Pfalz," the leadership ship of the brand-new warships of the Baden-Wuerttemberg party, has now been postponed until the first half of 2019, according to a military spokesman who interviewed Die Zeit newspaper.
The ship was supposed to have been picked up by the Navy in 2014, but serious problems after the delivery planned.
The four ships of the Baden-Wuerttemberg class ordered by the Navy in 2007 are intended to replace the Bremen-class warships that have come of age.
They are to be equipped with a powerful cannon, multiple anti-aircraft and ship missiles and camouflage technology such as reduced infrared radar and acoustic signals.
Other important features are a longer period of use - it should be possible to use the latest ships for up to two years away from their home ports.
However, constant delays mean that these warships of the latest type - which are said to enable Germany to show its strength at sea - will already become obsolete when they are finally put into operation, Die Zeit says.
The F125 warship made the news last year when the German military officially refused to take over and returned it to the Blohm & Voss slip in Hamburg.
This was the first time that the Navy has returned the ship back to the manufacturer after delivery.
Little was known about the reasons behind the divorce, but the German media talked about a variety of serious “flaws in software and hardware” that rendered the warship useless if it were deployed.
Defects in the software were particularly important as warships of the Baden-Wuerttemberg class will be controlled by 120 crew members, which is half the size of Bremen's warships.
It was also found that the ship was all too heavy, which reduces its performance and limits the Navy's ability to make additions to it in the future.
The 7000-tonship “Rheinland-Pfalz” is believed to be twice the size of a similar ship used by the Germans during World War II.
Aside from hardware flaws, the cost of the whole task - including crew training - is beginning to cause problems.
It is said to have reached 3.1 billion euros (3.6 billion dollars), but was originally supposed to be 2.2 billion euros.
Problems facing the latest ships are of particular importance in light of warnings that Germany's advantage at seas is declining.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, head of the Defence Committee of the German parliament, acknowledged that the Navy was in fact becoming a "dripping with operational vessels."
The agent said the problem had subsided over time, because older ships were taken out of service but no ships came in their place.
He lamented that none of the orders of the Baden-Wuerttemberg class could be put into use by the Navy.
The National Trust Conservations Association spies on the secret lives of bats
A new study carried out in the territory of the Scottish Highlands aims to find out how bats use the landscape as they hunt for food.
It is hoped that the results will shed new light on the behavior of these unique flying mammals and will help to guide conservation efforts in the future.
The study, conducted by Scientists at the National Trust for Scotland will monitor different types of bats in the Inverewe parks in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be installed at key locations around the area to monitor the behaviour of the bats over the period.
NHS staff and volunteers will also go on patrols with hand-held sensors.
Experts will audite all the recordings to find out the frequency of the calls, which the bats emit, and what types do what.
Maps across habitats and reports will then be released to create a detailed picture of their behavior on a geographical scale.
Rob Dewar, a nature conservation consultant for NTS, hopes the results reveal what kind of habitats are most important for the bats and how they are used by each type.
This information will help to determine the benefits of managing habitats, e.g. by creating meadows and how best to maintain woodland for bats and other related species.
There has been a significant decrease in the last century in Scotland and throughout the United Kingdom.
They are threatened by construction and development projects that affect their nests and decrease habitats.
Wind turbines and lights can also cause risks, including fly paper and certain chemical treatments of building materials, as well as attacks from domestic cats.
Bats aren't really blind.
On the other hand, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to hunting prey, because they are used to fishing at night.
They used advanced echo targeting techniques to find insects and objects that are on their way.
NTS, which is responsible for the maintenance of over 270 historical buildings, 38 important gardens and 76000 hectares of land all over the country, takes bats very seriously.
The organization employs ten experts, who carry out regular checks, nest inspections and sometimes rescue operations.
Scotland's first and only bat reserves have even been established at Threave's ranch in Dumfries and Galloway where eight of Scotland's ten bat species have a home.
David Thompson, the ranch coordinator, says he's the perfect area for them.
"Threave's area is great for bats," he said.
“We’ve got old buildings, lots of grown trees and lots of good habitats.
But there’s still a lot unknown about bats, so the work we do here and in other places will help us better understand what they need to thrive.”
He stressed the importance of checking bats before maintenance is carried out at dwellings, as the possible unintentional destruction of a single nest can kill up to 400 female bats and young and completely deplete all bats in the area.
Bats are protected and it is forbidden to kill, harass, or interfere, or destroy their nests.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish agent at the Bat Conservation Trust, has urged the public to help.
She said: "We still have a lot to learn about bats and we don't know how well how many of them are doing."
Ronaldo denies allegations of rape as lawyers prepare legal action against a German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo has summoned allegations against him of rape "fake news" and says people want to "raise their attention" using his name.
His lawyers plan to prosecute German news magazine Der Spiegel, which published the allegations.
The striker for Portugal and Juventus has been accused of raping an American woman named Kathryn Mayorga in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
He is said to have paid her 37,000 dollars afterwards for being silenced about the incident, according to Der Spiegel on Friday.
A few hours after the allegations were published, Ronaldo, 33, said in an Instagram live video to his 142 million followers that they were "fake news."
"No, no, no, no.
"What was said today is fake news," the world's champion said five times with the camera.
"These people want to draw attention to themselves by using my name.
That's normal.
It wants to become famous by using my name, but that's part of the job.
"I'm a happy man and everything is fine," added the player, smiling.
Ronaldo's lawyers prepare to prosecute Der Spiegel over the allegations, which they have called an "unacceptable disclosure of a suspicion concerning privacy," according to a Reuters news agency.
Lawyer Christian Schertz said Ronaldo would demand compensation for "an injury of the same amount as the severity of the offence, which is probably one of the most serious violations of personal rights in recent years."
The alleged incident is said to have occurred in June 2009 in a suite at the Palms hotel and the casino in Las Vegas.
After meeting at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga reportedly returned to the player's room, where he allegedly raped her in the rectum, according to documents filed with Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga said Ronaldo collapsed after the alleged incident and told her he was an "99 per cent good man" who fell into temptation because of "1 per century."
The documents claim that Ronaldo agreed that the couple had sex but that it was with the consent of both.
Mayorga claims she went to the police and photographed her injuries in a hospital, but finally agreed to reconciliation outside the courtroom because she was “deadly terrified of retaliation” and was worried about getting “degraded publicly.”
She says she now wants to cancel the settlement because she's still in shock after the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was close to joining Real Madrid from Manchester United when the rape was supposed to have taken place, and in the summer he crossed over to Italian giant Juve with a 100 million euro deal.
Brexit: Britain would 'see regret it indefinitely' losing automakers
The UK would "regret it indefinitely" if it lost its position as an international leader among carmakers after Brexit, Business Secretary Greg Clark said.
He added that it was "worrying" that Toyota UK had told the BBC that if the UK leaves the EU without a deal, the company would temporarily discontinue production at its factory in Burnaston, near Derby.
"We need a deal," Mr Clark said.
The Japanese carmaker said the impact of delays at the border if Brexit without a deal became a reality would cost jobs.
Burnaston, which manufactures Toyota Auris and Avensis vehicles, produced nearly 140,000 vehicles last year, 90% of which were exported to other EU countries.
"My opinion is that if the UK leave the EU at the end of March, we'll see production facilities at our factory," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's chief executive at Burnaston.
Other UK automakers have voiced concerns over leaving the EU without agreement on how international trade should be carried out, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
For example, BMW says it will close its Mini factory in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
The main concerns relate to what automakers say are supply chain risks if Brexit without a deal becomes a reality.
Toyota’s production line is based on ‘last-time’, where parts are delivered every 37 minutes from suppliers in both the United Kingdom and the EU for on-demand vehicles.
If the UK withdraws from the EU without a deal on 29 March, there could be disruptions at the border that the industry believes could lead to delays and shortages of components.
It would be impossible for Toyota to store more than a daily yeast at its factory in Derbyshire, the company said, and therefore production would stop.
Mr Clark said Theresa May's Chequers programme for a future relationship with the EU "is aimed specifically at preventing these views at the border."
"We have to have a contract. We want to get the best deal that allows us to enjoy not only success in the modern era, but as an opportunity we can take advantage of," he said on a radio show on BBC Radio 4.
“The evidence, not just from Toyota but from other manufacturers as well, shows that we need to be able to move forward with extremely effective resources.
Toyota couldn't say how long their production would stop, but warned that in the long term, increased costs would reduce the factory's competitiveness and ultimately cost jobs.
Peter Tsuvallaris, who has worked in Burnaston for 24 years and is a union representative at the factory, said his colleagues were increasingly concerned: "My experience is that when these jobs disappear, they will never come back.
A government spokeswoman said: "We've presented a detailed and comprehensive plan for our future relationship with the EU."
A meeting between Trump and Rosenstein could be postponed again, according to the White House.
The White House declared on Sunday that an important meeting between Donald Trump and Deputy Assistant Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could be "deferred again by one week" now the disagreement over Brett Kavanaugh, the nomined Supreme Court justice, continues.
Rosenstein oversees the work of Robert Muellers, a special prosecutor who investigates Russian elections, connections between Trump's and Russia's aides, and possible barriers to the advancement of justice at the hands of the president.
For weeks, there have been rumors in Washington about whether Trump will run deputy state prosecutors and thereby jeopardize Mueller's independence.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rostenstein had debated carrying a tape recorder to record conversations with Trump and the possibility of overthrowing the president using an additional Article 25 of the Constitution.
Rostenstein denied these claims.
But last Monday, he visited the White House, among the news reports that he was about to resign.
Instead, a meeting with Trump was announced on Thursday, but he was then at the United Nations in New York.
Trump said he "preferably" didn't want to fire Rosenstein, but then the meeting was postponed so that he wouldn't at the same time be heard by the Senate Judiciary Committee, where Kavanahugh and Dr. Christine Blasey Ford, one of the women who has sued him for sexual harassment, both testified.
On Friday, Trump ordered a week-long FBI investigation into allegations against Kavanaugh, which further postponed the Senate vote.
Trump's press secretary, Sarah Sander, was broadcast on Fox News Sunday.
When she was asked about the meeting with Rosenstein, she said: “A date hasn’t been set for him, it could happen this week. I think it’s possible that it will be postponed by another week because of everything else that’s going on at the Supreme Court.”
But we'll see, and it's always my pleasure to inform the media."
Some reporters disagree with this statement: Sanders hasn't held a press conference at the White House since September 10.
Chris Wallace asked the reason.
Sanders said a small number of meetings weren't due to dissatisfaction that television journalists had "virtualism," but she said: "I don't disagree with the fact that they're having pretense."
She then gave hints that the direct relationship between Trump and the media would increase.
"The president answers more questions from the media than any other president has done in the past," she said, without referring to sources: "We've reviewed those numbers."
"If the media has the opportunity to ask the President of the United States directly, it's much better than talking to me.
“We’re trying to do a lot of that, and you’ve seen us doing a lot of it over the last few weeks and it’s going to replace press conferences when you can talk to the President of the United States.”
Trump regularly answers questions when he leaves the White House or participates in open meetings or press conferences with other leaders who visit him.
Media meetings with him alone are rare.
The president perhaps revealed the reason in New York this week, when he exhibited rampant and strange behavior in front of a group of reporters.
Health minister writes EU staff within Scotland's health system over concerns about Brexit
The Minister of Health has written to staff from the EU who work within the health care system in Scotland (NHS Scotland) to express their appreciation and wish them to continue working after Brexit has gone through.
Jeane Freeman sent a letter in less than six months until Britain leaves the EU.
The Scottish government has already pledged to meet the costs of licensing applications for EU citizens working within the public service sector.
Miss Freeman wrote in his letter: "During the summer, negotiations between the UK and the EU have resumed and decision-making is expected in the autumn.
The UK government has also been working on preparations if there may not be a deal.
I know it's going to be a time of great uncertainty for all of you.
That's why I'd like to point out again how much I value the work of all the staff, no matter their nationality.
Colleagues from the EU and beyond bring valuable experience and knowledge that strengthens and improves the work of the health service and is for the benefit of the patients and the communities we serve.
Scotland is definitely your home and we want you to stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie underwent emergency surgery after suffering head injury
Christion Abercrombie, a lineman for the Tennessee State Tigers had to undergo emergency surgery after suffering a head injury in a 31-27 loss match against team Vanderbilt Commodores on Saturday, according to a report from Mike Organ, Tennessean's reporter.
Rod Reed, Tennessee State's head coach, told reporters that the injury had occurred shortly before half-time.
"He came to the sidelines and fell down there," Reed said.
Trainers and paramedics gave Abercrombie oxygen on the sidelines before being put on a stretcher and worn out for closer inspection.
A representative from the Tennessee State told Chris Harris, WSMV's broadcaster in Nashville, Tennesse, that Abercrombie was done with surgery at Vanderbilt's hospital.
Harris added that there was “no information about the type or severity of the injury yet” and that Tennessee State was trying to figure out when the accident occurred.
Abercrombie, who is in the final year of school, plays his first season with Tennessee State after moving away from Illinois.
He had five tackles in total on a Saturday before dropping out, which increased the total number of tackles with him to 18.
Foreign buyers will be charged higher stamp fees when they buy real estate in the UK
Foreign buyers will be charged higher stamp fees when they buy real estate in the UK. The excess money will be used to assist the homeless, according to Conservative Party's new plan
The action will make no impact of Corbyn's campaign to attract young voters
Increased stamp duty will be levied on those who do not pay tax in the UK
The Treasury plans to earn 120 million pounds a year - to help the homeless.
Foreign buyers will be charged higher stamp fees when they buy real estate in the UK. The excess money will be used to assist the homeless. Theresa May will announce this today.
The action will be considered an attempt to destroy Jeremy Corbyn's campaign to attract young voters by promising housing at affordable prices and further tax high-income earners.
Individuals and corporations who don't pay taxes in the UK will have to pay higher stamping fees, and the excess money will go into the government's actions to assist the homeless.
The increase - which is in addition to existing stamp duty fees, including the increase that was imposed two years ago on extra homes and real estate purchases for rent - could be up to three per cent.
The Treasury estimates that the increase will raise up to 120 million pounds a year.
It is estimated that 13 per cent of London's new buildings are being bought by non-residents, causing price hikes and making it more difficult for those who want to buy real estate for the first time to enter the market.
Many prosperous areas in the country - especially in the capital - have become "ghost towns" because of the number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time abroad.
The new strategy comes just a few weeks after Boris Johnson called for a reduction in stamp duty to help more young people acquire their first home.
He blamed large construction companies for keeping property prices high by buying land but not using them, urging May to cancel affordable household quotas to fix Britain's "housing shame."
Mr Corbyn has announced a number of promising improvements to housing issues, including rental supervision and the end of evictions from rental housing without charge.
Also, he wants to give advice more power to build new homes.
Theresa May said: "Last year I said I would devote myself to the restoration of the British dream in my first season - that every new generation should enjoy a better quality of life.
And that involves fixing our useless real estate market.
Britain's doors will always be open to those who want to live here, work and build their lives.
However, it may not be right that it is as easy for non-residents and companies headquartered abroad to buy homes as for hard-working British citizens.
“For many people, the dream of their own housing has become far too distant, and the shame that comes with homelessness is still too real.”
Jack Ross: "My main goal is to coach Scotland"
Sunderland's coach Jack Ross says it's his "main goal" to become Scotland's coach someday.
The 42-year-old Scot now enjoys the challenge of blowing new life to the North-East club which currently sits in third place in the first division, three points from the top.
He moved to Sunderland this summer after bringing St. Mirren back to the Scottish Premier League last season.
"I wanted to play for my country as a player.
“I injured my knee and it was over,” Ross told BBC Scotland’s Sportsound.
"But I grew up watching Scotland in Hampden many times with my dad, as a child, and there's always something that's pulled me back.
But that opportunity would only come if I did well to coach a league team.”
Ross coaches of Sunderland include Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O’Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former coach Alloa Athletic said he did not feel any fear of following in the footsteps of such famous names at such a large club, having previously declined coaching positions at Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
"Currently, I'm going to measure my success by thinking, 'Can I bring this club back to the Premier League?"
"This club is undoubtedly at home in the Premier League because of its structure and facilities," he said.
"It's not an easy task to get him there, but I'd probably only consider myself successful here if I put the club back up there."
Ross has only three years of experience, as a coach, after a season as an assistant coach at Dumbarton and 15 months as part of a coaching team for Hearts.
He then assisted Alloa in recovering from falling into a third division, transforming St Mirren, who was about to fail the league, into a league champion the following season.
Ross says he's more confident now than he ever was during his playing career at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
"It was probably a real turning point," he says of the season when he became Alloa's coach.
"I really believed the coaching staff suited me even more than playing.
That sounds weird, because I was doing well, I had a decent income from it and enjoyed good success.
However, changes can be difficult.
You have to go through a lot every week.
I'm still going through it in terms of stress and pressure away from the job, but the coaching job simply seems right for me.
“I’ve always wanted to coach, and now I’ve done it, I’ve never felt better about myself in my adult life.”
You can listen to the entire interview on Sportsound on Sunday, September 30 on Radio Scotland between 12.00 and 13.00 local time
The best time for beer is at 17.30 on Saturdays, according to an opinion poll
This summer's heat wave has proven to be lucrative for struggling British bars, while increasing pressure on restaurant chains.
Sales at pubs and bars increased by 2.7 percent in July, while sales at restaurants fell 4.8 percent, according to figures.
Peter Martin, from CGA's business advisory firm, compiling the figures, said: "Continuing sunshine and England's unexpected success in the World Cup meant that July followed a similar pattern as the month of June, when bar sales increased by 2.8 per cent, except that restaurants sold even less.
Sales fell by 1.8 per cent at restaurants in June and deteriorated again in July.
Pubs and bars, aimed mainly at beverage sales, did by far the best with more boom while restaurants went downwards.
Pubs who sold food also had a hard time in the sun, but not as much as restaurants.
It seems that people just wanted to go out for a drink.
The sale of beverages by pubs and bars increased by 6.6 per cent for the month, while food sales declined by three per cent.”
Paul Newman, from analytics firm RSM, said: "These results are a continuation of the swing we've seen since the end of April.
Weather and the impact from important social events or sporting events are still the biggest factors when it comes to sales in off-home markets.
It's not surprising that restaurant groups are still struggling, although sales reductions of 4.8 per cent month-on-month are particularly painful on top of ongoing pressures caused by costs.
“This long and hot summer could hardly come at a worse time for diners and only time will tell whether temperate temperatures, such as we have had in August, will improve the situation.”
The overall growth in sales by pubs and restaurants, including in newly opened venues, was 2.7 per cent in July, reflecting how the openings have slowed.
Coffer Peach Tracker, the monitoring company on sales in the British pub bar and restaurant industry, gathers and analyzes data from 47 operating groups, with a combined turnover of 9 billion pounds and is the industry benchmark for the sector.
One in five children have secret accounts on social media that they hide from their parents
One in five children, as young as 11, have secret social media accounts that they hide from their parents and teachers, according to a survey results.
An opinion poll covering 20,000 students showed an increase in “artificial Insta” pages
The news has exacerbated concerns about sexual content being published
Twenty percent of students claim to have a "main" account they show to parents
One in five children, as young as 11 years old, create secret accounts on social media that they keep secret to adults.
An opinion poll covering 20,000 students showed a huge increase in “artificial Insta” accounts – which is a reference to Instagram’s website.
The news has exacerbated concerns that sexual content is being published.
Twenty percent of the students said they had a clean “main” account to show their parents, as well as having secret accounts.
One mother, who dropped out of her 13-year-old daughter's secret website, found a teenager urging others to "rape me."
The study, which was conducted by the Digital Awareness UK and the Assembly of Private Principals (HMC), showed that 40 per cent of children aged 11-18 had two accounts and half of them admitted to having secret accounts.
Mike Buchanan, chairman of HMC, said: "It is worrying that so many teenagers are tempted to create areas online where parents and teachers can't find them."
Eilidh Doyle becomes "the voice of athletes" on the Scottish Athletics Sports Commission
Eilidh Doyle has been elected to the Scottish Athletics Committee as head without executive power at the Board's annual plenary session.
Doyle is Scotland's most successful athletic athlete and Ian Beattie's chairman said the nomination was a great opportunity for those who led the sport to benefit from her extensive international experience over the past decade.
"Eilidh is highly respected within sports communities in Scotland, the UK and internationally, and we're confident that sports in Scotland would make a lot of money getting it on the committee," Beattie said.
Doyle said: "I am very interested to speak on behalf of athletes and hope that I can contribute and help lead the sport in Scotland."
