He tweeted: "One month ago I had a stroke.
I couldn't walk.
Doctors said that only time would tell if I recovered and to what extent.
It's been a hard job but I fully recovered, learned to walk again and do sports training today!
“Thank you for your encouragement!”
Breast pump advertisement, which compares mothers with cows, causes disputes online
A company that manufactures breast pumps is now being talked about online because of an advertisement that carries lactating mothers to cows that are milked.
Elvie published a witty advertisement to promote what is said to be “the world’s first quiet breast pump that can be carried around”. The advertisement is inspired by music videos and is supposed to show the freedom that the new pump provides to breastfeeding mothers.
Four real mothers dance in a barn full of hay and cuddly to a song with lyrics such as: “Yes, I milk myself, but you don’t see my tail” and “If you didn’t notice it, it’s not udder, it’s my breasts.”
“Pump, pump, I’ll nourish the children, pump, pump, I’m pumping milk out of the chickens.”
However, the ad, which appeared on the company's Facebook page, has caused online publicity.
The video features 77,000 views and hundreds of remarks and has received varying reactions from viewers, many of whom say she jokes with the "horror" attached to the dairy industry.
“It’s a really bad decision to use cows to advertise this product.
Just like us, they have to become calf full and carry to produce milk, except that the calves are taken from them just a few days after they are born,” he said.
Elvie breast pump fits comfortably into a breastfeeding holder (Elvie/Mother)
Another observer wrote: "Undestibly difficult for both mother and child.
“Whyn’t we use them to advertise breast pumps for mothers who are allowed to keep their babies?”
Another added: "This advertisement is totally out of tune with the times."
Others defended the advertisement and one woman acknowledged that she thought the song was "hilarious."
"I think it's a brilliant idea.
I'd have bought something like this if I was still breastfeeding.
I felt exactly like a cow when I was pumping my milk.
It's a bit crazy, but I accepted it as it is.
"It's a brilliant product," one of the spectators wrote.
Another said: “It’s an amusing advertisement that’s directed at mothers pumping milk (often at work or inside the toilet) and feels like ‘cuddly.’
It's not an advertisement that compliments or judges the dairy industry."
At the end of the announcement, the women's team shows that they were all dancing with their breast pumps hidden in their bras.
The idea behind the advertising campaign is based on the fact that many women say they feel like cows when they pump out milk.
However, the Elvie pump is quite quiet, has no wires or pipes and fits comfortably into the bra, which gives women the freedom to move, hold their baby and even go out while pumping.
Ana Balarin, co-owner and manager at Mother, said: "Elvie the pump is such a revolutionary product that she deserved a prominent presentation that would provoke publicity.
By imitating women with cows, we wanted to turn the spotlight on breast pumping and all its challenges, and show even in a fun and familiar way how much freedom the new pumpan would provide.
This isn't the first time Elvie Pumpan has made the news.
During London Fashion Week, a mother of two appeared at a fashion show for designer Marta Jakubowski while using the product.
Hundreds of immigrant children moved quietly to tents on the Texas border
The number of migrants in custody has increased abruptly even though the number of people crossing the border monthly has remained roughly the same. Part of the reason is that the harsh rhetoric and policies put forward by the Trump administration have made it harder to get the children into temporary foster care.
Usually the foster parents have been unregistered immigrants themselves, and have feared to put their chances of staying in the country at risk, by volunteering to foster a child.
The risk increased in June, when public authorities announced that potential foster parents and other adults within the home would have to provide fingerprints, and that the data would be shared with the immigration regulator.
Last week, Matthew Albence, an officer within the immigration and customs regulator, testified in front of the Senate and said his organization had arrested a number of people who applied to foster single children.
The agency later confirmed that 70 percent of those arrested did not have a criminal record.
“Nearly 80 percent of those who are either foster parents or inside a home that fosters children, is in the country illegal, and a large part of them are illegal immigrants on the criminal record.
"That's why we continue to look for those individuals," Mr Albence said.
In an attempt to process the children's cases more quickly, representatives announced new rules that demand that some of the children appear in the courtroom within a month from the time they were detained, rather than after 60 days, which was the previous criteria, according to staff working in the shelters.
Many of them will attend through a video meeting, rather than in person, to argue for their legal status faced by immigration referees.
Children who are judged unfit to enter the country are quickly deported.
The longer children are detained, the more likely they are to become anxious or depressed, which can lead to violent or refugee attempts, according to shelter employees and reports published by the system in recent months.
Advocates said these problems were even more likely in a larger institution such as in Tornillo, where it is likely that no one notices it even though a child is having a difficult time, due to how many there are.
They added that the children's transport to the tent city without providing enough time to prepare them mentally or greeting friends could exacerbate the trauma that many of them already struggle with.
Syria says "U.S., France and Turkey" are withdrawing immediately
Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem also said in his speech to the UN General Assembly that Syrian refugees should return home, even though the country's war is now in its eighth year.
Moualem, who also serves as deputy prime minister, said that foreign forces were illegal in Syria, under the false premise that they fought against terrorism, and that "they will be treated accordingly."
"They have to withdraw immediately and without any conditions," he told Congress.
Moualem firmly argued that "terrorist war was almost over" in Syria, where over 360,000 people have died since 2011 and millions have had to flee their homes.
He said Damascus would continue "to wage this sacred struggle until we clean up all the Syrian regions" by both terrorist groups and "all illegal foreign agents."
The United States employs about 2000 troops in Syria, where most of them train and advise both Kurdish forces and Syrian Arabs who fight against President Bashar al-Assad.
France has over 1000 troops in this war-torn country.
As far as refugee issues are concerned, Moualem said conditions were good enough that they could return and he blamed “someth of Western countries for “spreading out unfoundeded fears” that encouraged refugees to stay away.
"We've called on a global community and humanitarian organization to facilitate their return," he said.
“They create politics out of something that should be purely humanitarian.”
The United States and the European Union have warned that they will not provide any funding for reconstruction in Syria unless there is a political agreement between Assad and the Resilience to end the war.
According to U.S. diplomats, a recentive treaty between Russia and Turkey to set up a neutral area at the last stronghold of resistance created an opportunity to continue political dialogue.
The agreement between Russia and Turkey prevented a massive attack from Syrian troops sponsored by Russia, to a province where three million people live.
Moualem, however, stressed that the deal had a "clear final deadline" and expressed hope that military action would be aimed at jihadists, including fighters from Nusra Front, who have ties to Al-Qaeda, and that they "would be eradicated."
UN Ambassador Staffan de Mistura hopes to arrive at the first meetings with a new committee consisting of members of the government and the resistance, to draft a constitution for Syria after the war and pay the street for elections.
Moualem set out the conditions for the Syrian government's participation in the committee, and said its work should be limited to "revising the provisions of the current government," and warned of all interventions.
