Money on the outside flows into the narrowest elections of the middle season
It's not surprising that Pennsylvania's 17th electoral area sees the money flowing, thanks to restructuring in polling areas which led to two delegates competing for the same seat.
The newly restructured Pittsburg area sends out Democrat Conor Lamb, who won his seat in another electoral area in a special election last summer.
Lamb is running against another Republican Keith Rothfus, who now represents the 12th electoral territory in Pennsylvania, which overlaps heavily with the new 17 polling area.
The maps of the electoral territory were restructured after Pennsylvania's Supreme Court ruled in January that the old electoral territories were organized in favour of Republicans and that it violated the constitution.
The race in the new 17 electoral area has been the beginning of a competition in the funding of election campaigns between the main funding bodies of both parties, the Democratic Electoral Commission (DCCC) and the Election Committee of the Republicans (NRCC).
Lamb became a familiar name in Pennsylvania after a narrow victory in a special election in March for the 18th electoral zone in Pennsylvania, many of whom followed that election.
Republicans had previously warmed that place for more than a decade and President Donald Trump won in the polling station with a 20-point difference.
Political analysts have given Democrats a bit of an advantage.
The U.S. considered punishing El Salvador because of support for China, but then dropped out.
State diplomats noticed that the Dominican Republic and Panama had already acknowledged Beijing, with little objection from Washington.
Mr. Trump had a warm meeting with Panama's President Juan Carlos Varela in June 2017 and stayed in a hotel in Panama until co-owners expelled Trump's management team.
Representatives of the State Department decided to call back the U.S. ambassador to El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama because of a "recental decision not to recognize Taiwan anymore," the ministry's spokeswoman Heather Nauert said in a statement early this month.
However, punishments were contemplated solely against El Salvador, which is estimated to have received US$140 million for relief efforts in 2017, including for drug control, development efforts and economic support.
The penalties, which were proposed and included, among other things, reductions in financial support and restrictions on the issuance of residence permits, would have been painful for the Central American country, where high unemployment prevails and the murder rate is high.
As in-house meetings continued, U.S
But in mid-September, representatives from the top ranks made it clear that they wanted the conference to take place, and consequently ended all speculation about punishments against El Salvador.
Vice-President Mike Pence is currently addressing the conference, which is due to take place in mid-October, as a symbol of how important it is in the eyes of the administration, the diplomats said.
U.S. diplomats also quietly returned to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic, with no new harsh messages or punishments from Washington.
A White House spokesman for Mr Bolton declined to further discuss the discussions described by the three U.S. delegates, including two diplomats, who agreed to discuss the in-house arguments against being allowed to remain anonymous.
Their descriptions were confirmed by an outside analyst who works closely with the board and also talked about the case against anonymity.
Let’s learn the story
The next scandal could be a report by Robert Mueller, the special prosecutor, about Mr. Trump's possible actions to obstruct justice, but for that accusation there's a variety of public evidence.
It is also said that Mr. Mueller reversed his investigation into whether Mr. Trump's election campaign worked with Russia in its attack on our election.
If Congress is governed by another party, Trump may be held accountable, as he prepares to meet his voters again, and perhaps a jury appointed his peers.
This, however, is extremely uncertain, and I do not want to suggest that Trump's fall is unequivocal, nor the fall of his colleagues in Europe.
We all have to choose, on both sides of the Atlantic, which will affect how long the battle will be.
In 1938, German rulers were willing to kidnap Hitler, only if Western countries had resisted him and supported the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
We failed and missed the opportunity to avoid the catastrophic years that followed.
The story revolves around such important points and accelerates or delays the unstoppable progress of democracy.
The U.S. faces some such important points now.
What do we do if Mr. Trump fires Rod Rosenstein, the man in charge of the fate of Mr. Mueller's investigation?
Rosenstein has been in a bad position since this paper reported that last year he had proposed making secret recordings of the president and discussed whether he was incompetent in office.
Mr. Rosenstein says the Times story isn't right.
"How will we respond if the newly requested FBI investigation into Brett Kavanaugh is not thorough or fair, or if it is upheld in the seat of a Supreme Court judge despite likely allegations of sexual harassment and dishonest testimony?
And above all, will we vote for Congress in the middle season that will let Mr. Trump take responsibility?
If we fail these tests, democracy will have to last a long winter.
But I don't think we'll fall, because of the lesson I learned in Prague.
My mother was Jewish from Czechoslovakia and was sent to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime that previously sat at my ambassador's residence.
She survived, moved to the United States and 60 years later she sent me to light Sabbath candles on the table with the swastika.
With such a legacy, how can I other than look positively to our future?”
Norman Eisen, a member of the Brookings Institution, is also chairman of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington and the author of "The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House."
Graham Dorran's teammaster Rangers, optimistic for the game against Rapid Vienna
Rangers take on Rapid Vienna on Thursday, knowing that victory against the Austrians will bring them into a strong position to get out of the G-squad of the Europa League. Rangers made an impressive draw in Spain against Villarreal earlier this month.
Injuries on the knee prevented central defender Graham Dorrans from playing his first game of the season earlier in the game against Villareal which ended in a 2-2 draw, but he believes Rangers can take advantage of those results as a springboard for better results.
"It was good to get this point for us, because Villarreal is a strong team," Dorrans said.
"We started the game with the belief that we could get something and ended up taking one point.
Maybe we could have squeezed out a win at the end, but overall the draw was probably a fair result.
They were probably better in the first half and we came in strong in the second half and played better.
On Thursday it's another crucial night for the Europa League.
Hopefully we can get three points but it will be a tough game because they did well in their last game. But with spectators who support us, I'm sure we'll endure this and get positive results.
Last year was difficult, both because of my injuries and the changes that occurred in the club itself, but now there is a good feeling on the spot.
The team is good and the boys enjoy themselves, the training is good.
Hopefully we can go further now, put last season behind us and make progress.”
Women lose sleep because of fear of losing retirement savings
Although participants in the opinion poll had a clear idea of how they wanted to be cared for, few people talked to their family members about it.
About half of the individuals in the Nationwide survey said they spoke to their spouses about the cost of long-term care.
Only 10 percent said they would discuss this with their children.
"People want a family member to take care of it, but it doesn't take the right steps to have that conversation," said Holly Snyder, deputy director of Nationwide's life insurance department.
That's how to get started.
Talk to your partner and your children: You cannot prepare your family to provide care if you don't make your wishes clear in good time.
Discuss with your counselor and your family about where and when you receive care, because these options can change a lot in terms of costs.
Get your financial adviser involved in the case: Your adviser can also assist you in finding a way to pay for these costs.
Among the options you have to pay for long-term care are traditional life insurance with long-term care, mixed life insurance that pays out in cash, to help with paying costs, or self-insurance with your own money – as long as you own it.
Prepare all legal documents: Prevent legal disagreements in advance.
Get a guarantor for your health care so that you can nominate someone you trust to oversee your health care and ensure that health care professionals comply with your wishes if you can’t tell them directly yourself.
Also, consider getting procurators for your finances.
You'd choose someone you trust to make financial decisions on your behalf and make sure that your bills are paid if you can't do it yourself.
Don't forget the details: Imagine that your elderly father or mother is in an emergency and going to the hospital.
Could you answer questions about medications and allergies?
Give that information exactly in a written plan so that you are ready.
"It's not just about the finances, it's also about who the doctors are," Martin said.
"What's the medication?
Who's going to take care of the dog?
Get that plan ready.”
A man shot multiple times with an air gun in Ilfracombe
A man was shot multiple times with an air gun as he was on his way home on foot after being out having fun.
The victim is a man in his 50s and was in the Oxford Grove area of Ilfracombe, Devon, when he was shot in the chest, abdomen and hand.
Representatives describe the shooting, which occurred around 02.30 a.m. local time, as "a random attack."
The victim didn't see his attacker.
The man was not seriously injured and police have called for witnesses.
Earthquakes and tidal waves in Indonesia
At least 384 people were killed after a powerful earthquake and tsunami in the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, representatives said, and the number of dead is expected to rise.
Since all communication lies down, auxiliary personnel have not been able to provide any information from Donggala county, which is an area north of Palu and lies closer to the center of the earthquake, which was 7.5 on Richter.
More than 16,000 people were evacuated in Palu's home after misfortune struck.
Here are some key facts about Palu and Donggala, on the island of Sulawesi:
Palu is the capital of the province's central Sulawesi province and is located at the bottom of a cramped bay on the west coast of the island of Sulawesi. The estimated number of inhabitants was 377,800 in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when the earthquake and tsunami hit.
Donggala is a county that covers more than 380 km of coastline in the northwestern island of Sulawesi.
The county, which is a control area under a province, had an estimated population of 299,200 in 2017.
Fisheries and agriculture are the main industries in the Mid-Sulawesi region, especially in Donggala's coastal areas.
Nickel mines are also important in the province, but most of them are in Morowali, which is on the beach opposite Sulawesi.
Tsunamis have hit Palu and Donggala several times in the last century, according to Indonesia's catastrophic establishment.
In 1938, more than 200 people died when a tsunami struck and hundreds of houses in Donggala were destroyed.
There was also a tsunami on the western side of Donggali in 1996, in which nine people died.
Indonesia sits on the active ring of fire of the Pacific Ocean and there are regular earthquakes.
Here are the major earthquakes and tsunamis in recent years:
2004: A major earthquake on the west coast of Aceh province in northern Sumatra struck on 26 December and triggered a tsunami that struck 14 countries and killed 226,000 people across the coastline of the Indian Ocean, of which more than half were in Aceh.
2005: A series of strong quakes swarmed over the west coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
Hundreds are killed on Nias Island, off the coast of Sumatra.
206: An Earthquake of 6.8 on the Richter struck southern Java, Indonesia's most populous island, causing a tsunami that struck the south coast and killed nearly 700 people.
2009: An Earthquake of 7.6 on Richter struck near the city of Padang, which is the capital of a province in western Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: An Earthquake of 7.5 on Richter struck one of the Mentawai islands near Sumatra, raising up to a 10-metre-high tidal wave that destroyed a number of villages and killed around 300 people.
2016: A shallow earthquake struck Pidie Jaya County in Aceh and caused destruction and panic as people remembered the destruction in the deadly Earthquakes and tsunami in 2004.
No tidal waves formed this time, but more than 100 were killed when buildings collapsed.
2018: Large quakes swarmed across Indonesia's tourist island Lombok and more than 500 people were killed, mostly in the northern part of the island.
The earthquake destroyed hundreds of buildings and thousands of tourists were trapped temporarily on the island.
Sarah Palin's eldest son arrested on charges of domestic violence
Track Palin, the eldest son of Sarah Palin, the former Alaska governor and presidential candidate, has been arrested on charges of assault.
Palin, a 29-year-old who from Wasilla in Alaska, was arrested on suspicion of domestic violence for trying to obstruct the reporting of domestic violence and stressing against arrest, according to a report from Alaska State Police, which was published on Saturday.
According to a police report, Palin took the phone from a friend when she tried to call the police to report an alleged offence.
Palin is being held at the Mat-Su station where he is awaiting a hearing in court, and he is being held against a 500-dollar bail, according to a report from KTUU.
He appeared in court on Saturday, declaring that he was "not guilty, firmly" when asked to declare guilt or innocence, according to the news channel.
Palin is charged with three offences in category A, which means he could be sentenced to prison for up to one year and had to pay 250,000 dollars fine.
He has also been charged with Class B offences, which is punishable by one day in prison and a 2000-dollar fine.
This isn't the first time Palin has been charged.
In December 2017, he was charged with assaulting his father, Todd Palin.
His mother, Sarah Palin, called the police to report the alleged attack.
The case is currently being treated for Alaska's surrender military court.
In January 2016, he was charged with domestic violence, blocking reports of domestic violence and carrying weapons under the influence of alcohol in connection with the incident.
His girlfriend has claimed he punched her in the face.
Sarah Palin was criticized by groups of surrender soldiers in 2016 after she blamed post-traumatic stress disorder about her son's violent behavior, after his military presence in Iraq.
