Prosecutors seek a rare death penalty for a suspected terrorist attacker in New York City
Prosecutors in New York want the death penalty over Sayfullo Saipov, who is alleged to have been involved in a terrorist attack in New York City that killed eight people. This is a rare punishment that has not been used in the state for violations of federal law since 1953.
Saipov, a 30-year-old, is believed to have taken a Home Depot truck leased to attack people on a bike path along the West Side Highway's street in the lower half of Manhattan. He drove there down pedestrians and cyclists in October.
To justify the death penalty, prosecutors must prove Saipov killed eight victims “intentionally” and caused serious physical injuries “intentionally,” according to the letter of intent to apply the death penalty, which was documented in New York’s Southern District Judgment.
