The reasons why a trade war between the U.S. and China could be good for Beijing
The first shots in the trade war between the United States and China were defamatory, and although the war is far from over, a rift between the two countries could be favorable for Beijing in the long run, analysts say.
U.S. President Donald Trump fired the first warning shot earlier this year by imposing a tax on China's major export products, including solar cells, steel and aluminum.
The greatest escalation came this week with new tariffs covering goods worth US$200 billion (150 billion pounds), and taxing almost half of all goods imported into the United States from China.
Beijing has responded in the same coin each time and recently imposed 5 to 10 percent tariffs on U.S. goods worth US$60 billion.
China has vowed to respond to U.S
To get Washington to retreat, claims need to be indulged, but it would be far too humiliating for Chinese President Xi Jinping to officially bend ahead of the United States.
However, analysts say that if Beijing holds the cards correctly, pressure from the trade war with the U.S. could have a positive impact on China in the long run, by reducing the mutual need of both economies for each other.
"The fact that a hasty political decision either in Washington or Beijing could lead to a situation that leads to economic collapses in either country, is actually much more dangerous than viewers have previously acknowledged," Abigail Grace, a researcher focused mainly on Asia at the New American Security Center.
Syria 'reconciled' for refugees to return, foreign minister said
Syria says it is ready for refugees to return willingly and seek help to rebuild the country, which is now in bad condition after a seven-year-long war.
Walid al Moualem, the foreign minister, spoke at the United Nations General Assembly and said the situation in the country would improve.
"Today, the situation on the ground is more stable and safer, thanks to success in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government continues to establish ordinary conditions by building areas that were completely destroyed by terrorists.
Everything is now prepared for migrants to return willingly to the country they were forced to leave because of terrorism and unilateral economic decisions aimed against their daily lives and employment.
The UN estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have fled the country since the war began in 2011.
An additional six million people, who still live in the country, need assistance.
Al-Moualem said Syrian authorities would gladly accept assistance in rebuilding the country.
He stressed, however, that they would not accept aid under conditions or assistance from countries that supported the uprising.
Europe wins Ryder trophy in Paris
Europe's team has beaten the Ryder Cup in 2018 by defeating the U.S. team with a final result of 16.5 against 10.5 points on the Le Golf National course outside Paris in France.
The United States has now lost six times in a row on European soil and has not won the Ryder trophy in Europe since 1993.
Europe regained the throne when the team, led by Danish captain Thomas Bjorn, achieved the 14.5 points it needed to beat the United States.
Phil Mickelson, who struggled for most of the competition, made his initial shot into the water on the 16th hole, which is par 3, and lost his match against Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari shone brilliantly in all of his games and became 1 of 4 players to get the result 5-0-0, since the competition began using its current format in 1979.
Jordan Spieth lost 5&4 against Thorbjorn Olesen from Denmark, but he was at the bottom of the ranking of the players in the European team.
Dustin Johnson, who is at the top of the list of the world's best golfers, lost 2 and 1 against Ian Poulter of England who played possibly in his last Ryder Cup competition.
Spaniard Sergio Garcia, who has played eight times for the Ryder Cup, became the most victorious European of the competition with 25.5 career points.
